# Benchmarks

Three ways to pack the same repository, measured on this repository itself. The numbers are generated
by [`scripts/benchmark.mjs`](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/scripts/benchmark.mjs), which
anyone can run:

```bash
node scripts/benchmark.mjs /path/to/repo "the question to slice for"
```

The run below used the default question, on the repository at 0.12.34: 313 files, 987,750 estimated
tokens in full.

| Mode | Files | Parts | Tokens | vs full | Time |
| :-- | --: | --: | --: | --: | --: |
| full | 313 | 11 | 987,750 | 100% | 1.66 s |
| slice | 4 | 1 | 66,430 | 6.7% | 1.57 s |
| budget (30,000) | 21 | 1 | 36,315 | 3.7% | 1.55 s |

## What the columns mean

- **Files** — entries in `manifest.files`, i.e. what the parts actually carry.
- **Parts** — part files written. A budget/slice snapshot tends to be one part because it fits.
- **Tokens** — `manifest.totals.estimatedTokens`, the same estimate the product reports. It is one
  token per four bytes of emitted body, not a tokenizer count; every model tokenizes differently.
- **vs full** — token share of the full snapshot.
- **Time** — wall clock for the whole `generate` process, including scan, parse, partition and write.

## What to take from it

1. **The full snapshot is usually too large to paste.** Under a million tokens for this repository is
   typical for a mature codebase; a 200k-token window cannot hold it.
2. **A slice is two orders of magnitude smaller and still coherent**: four files that the planner
   judged relevant, with their bodies intact.
3. **A budget is the "fit the window" answer**: 21 whole files at 3.7% of the tokens, chosen by entry
   point, importer count and layer rather than by alphabetical order.
4. **Generation is not the bottleneck.** All three modes take about the same time; the saving is in
   what gets written, not in what gets read.

## Honest limits

- Self-benchmark. This repository is TypeScript with a clean import graph; a vendored monorepo with
  a thousand dependencies will score differently, and a repository with no imports at all gives the
  planner little to rank.
- One machine, one run. The times are indicative, not a controlled benchmark; the token counts are
  deterministic for a fixed repository and configuration.
- These are **our** modes, not a head-to-head against other tools. A comparison needs the same
  repository, the same budget and both tools' own token accounting, and that is a separate exercise.
</file>

<file path="docs/brief.md">
# The brief: the half only you can write

`PROMPT.md` is generated, and part of it cannot be. The manifest can prove which files are here,
which were skipped and why, what the system map is and what the standard requires. It cannot know
what the software is *for*, what has already gone wrong, or which failures this team would
recognise. That is judgement, so it is supplied as a file and merged into the brief.

```bash
# init writes BRIEF.md next to the config, with the five headings and guidance in comments
npx codebase-snapshot init --yes

# then enable it with one edit, or name your own file on the command line
npx codebase-snapshot generate --brief docs/brief.md
```

```js
// snapshot.config.mjs
export default {
  brief: "BRIEF.md",        // relative to the project root; a missing file is an error
  promptTask: "audit",      // audit | review | explain
};
```

## The five headings

| Heading | What happens |
| :--- | :--- |
| `## Stakes` | rendered as its own section, right after the job |
| `## The system` | replaces the generated narrative; the measured facts still follow under "What the index records" |
| `## What came before` | rendered above the generated commit ledger |
| `## The bar` | replaces the standard |
| `## Calibration` | replaces the example failures |

Any other `##` heading is kept, verbatim, under **Owner notes**. Nothing is dropped because the
merge did not recognise it — a brief is your text, and silently discarding a section is the one
thing this tool does not do.

Every owner section is marked in `PROMPT.md` as written by the project owner, so a reader can tell
an assertion from a measurement.

## A worked example

```markdown
<!-- BRIEF.md: fill in what you know; an empty section is simply not rendered -->

## Stakes

Runs live production for one owner. A wrong cleanup deletes their data, and the backup is taken by
the same process it is protecting.

## What came before

Four rounds, 23 defects. The recurring shapes: a guard at the route but not the timer, and an `ok`
flag that means only that the call returned.

## Calibration

- a grant signed for one recipe executing another recipe's deletions
- a recipe that never worked, because its test supplied a number where the daemon sends a string
```

## What is refused, and why

Three things are errors rather than overrides, because each would make the document lie:

- **a measured heading.** `Coverage`, `This snapshot`, `Attaching this`, `The job`, `What you have`
  and the rest are rendered from the manifest. Redefining one would let prose overwrite a
  measurement.
- **a file that cannot be read.** A snapshot must not quietly ship without the half you wrote.
- **an edited brief after generating.** Its digest is recorded, so `validate` reports
  `brief: docs/brief.md changed since this snapshot was generated`.

Two smaller ones: a heading defined twice is ambiguous and refused, and a file over 64 KiB is
refused with its size — past that it is a second codebase rather than instruction.

## How the file is parsed

| Input | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| `## stakes`, `## STAKES` | the stakes section; headings match case-insensitively |
| `## Stakes:` | the stakes section; a trailing colon is a typo, not a different heading |
| `<!-- ... -->` | removed before parsing — the `init` scaffold is all comments and renders nothing |
| a section with no text | not rendered at all |
| a fenced block containing `## Coverage` | content, not a heading |
| CRLF, or a UTF-8 byte-order mark | read like the plain case |
| a control character | escaped, as everywhere else this tool reproduces text |

## The job changes, the standard does not

`promptTask` changes only the opening section:

- `audit` (the default) — find what is still wrong or will break;
- `review` — say what a change breaks, and what it claims but does not do;
- `explain` — explain how a subsystem works, and cite the lines.

The standard, the coverage duty and the answer shape are the same in all three. An unknown value
is a usage error, never a silent fall back to the default.

## Relationship to the rest of the snapshot

`brief` is instruction, not payload: changing it does not change the files that are copied, but it
does change the config digest, so a stale brief is flagged exactly like a stale snapshot. The
writer’s prose is masked like every other reproduced string, and the brief file itself is an
ordinary file in the repository — it may be snapshotted, or excluded with `excludePatterns`.
</file>

<file path="docs/delta.md">
# Delta packs

```bash
codebase-snapshot generate --delta ./snapshot --out-dir ./snapshot-delta
```

A delta pack is the snapshot you would want for "what changed?" — the files that changed since a
base snapshot, plus the files that import them, and nothing else. The rest are accounted for rather
than dropped.

## What it does

1. Reads the base `manifest.json` (a directory or the file itself).
2. Hashes the current tree as usual and compares **content hashes**, not mtimes.
3. Keeps: added files, changed files, and the **one-hop importers** of those files.
4. Drops: unchanged files. Each goes into `skipped[]` with reason `unchanged` and into
   `delta.unchanged`, so the accounting rule still holds — nothing disappears without a record.
5. Records the comparison in the manifest:

```json
"delta": {
  "base": { "path": "/repo/snapshot/manifest.json", "fingerprint": "…", "generatedAt": "…" },
  "added": ["src/new.ts"],
  "changed": ["src/user.ts"],
  "removed": ["src/gone.ts"],
  "unchanged": ["package.json", "src/unrelated.ts"]
}
```

The brief (`PROMPT.md`) states the delta at the top, with the counts and the note that unchanged
files are not in the parts — so a model reading it cannot describe them from memory.

## Why it matters

A full snapshot of a large repository is mostly text the reader already has. A delta is small
enough to send on every turn, and it carries the two things a change review actually needs: the
new bytes and the callers that might break.

## Limits, stated

| Limit | Value | Why |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Base format | a manifest from this tool | The shape is checked; a foreign JSON is refused with the reason. |
| Importer closure | one hop | The same rule as `--with-dependents`; deeper closure is a graph query, not a pack. |
| Removed files | listed, not shown | The bytes are gone; the manifest can only say which paths disappeared. |
| Unchanged files | listed in `delta.unchanged` and `skipped[]` | Dropping them silently would break the accounting contract. |

A delta against a base from a different repository is not an error: nothing matches, so every file
is "added". The base fingerprint is recorded, and comparing it with the current one is the reader's
job.
</file>

<file path="docs/install.md">
# Installation

`codebase-snapshot` is published to the public npm registry, so it installs with
**any** npm-compatible client. There is no second registry to add and nothing to configure.

Requires **Node.js 18 or newer**.

## One command

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot --autopilot
```

Detects the project, writes the config (an existing one is kept), adds the npm scripts and the
devDependency, installs them with the package manager your lockfile names, and generates the first
snapshot. `--no-install` skips the install; `--dry-run` writes nothing. `setup` does the same as a
command word, and the bare `snapshot` (or `npx codebase-snapshot` with no arguments) does the
same after one question — see [the first run](#the-first-run). The sections below are the same
steps taken one at a time.

## The first run

With the package installed globally, the whole thing is one word:

```bash
snapshot
```

In a project with no configuration it names what it recognised and what it keyed on
(`Detected nextjs` / `Because next.config.ts`), then offers the recommended setup in one
keystroke or a walk through each setting with the defaults pre-filled. It writes the config,
adds the npm scripts and devDependency, installs, and generates.

In a project that already has a config — or one it has generated before — the same command just
generates, so nobody is walked through setup twice.

**It never guesses.** With no terminal (CI, a container, an agent) a first run exits `2` and
names the three ways forward instead of generating with settings nobody chose. `--autopilot` is
the answer for unattended use; naming the command (`npx codebase-snapshot generate`) is the
other, and it runs on the documented defaults exactly as before.

## One-off use

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot generate
```

This is the shortest path and needs no install.

Pin the version when you need the same build on every machine. `--version` prints the exact
string to pin:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot@latest generate
npx codebase-snapshot --version
```

**Why the bare name works.** npm resolves which binary to run from the package's `bin`
entries. This package declares `codebase-snapshot` and `snapshot` pointing at the *same file*, so
npm treats them as aliases and can always resolve one. Keep them aliases: two *different*
executables under a scoped package name would make the bare form unresolvable.

If a command exits with no output at all, a stale global install is the usual cause — see
[Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting).

## Add to a project

```bash
npm install -D codebase-snapshot
yarn add -D codebase-snapshot        # yarn classic and Berry
pnpm add -D codebase-snapshot
bun add -d codebase-snapshot
```

Then run it through your client so the binary resolves from the project:

| Client | Run |
| :--- | :--- |
| npm | `npx codebase-snapshot generate` |
| yarn (classic and Berry) | `yarn codebase-snapshot generate` |
| pnpm | `pnpm exec codebase-snapshot generate` |
| bun | `bun run codebase-snapshot generate` |

`snapshot` works everywhere `codebase-snapshot` does — `npx snapshot` from a project dependency,
`pnpm exec snapshot`, `yarn snapshot`, and the bare word after a global install. Adding the script
to `package.json` is the most reproducible option:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "snapshot": "npx codebase-snapshot generate",
    "snapshot:validate": "npx codebase-snapshot validate"
  }
}
```

`codebase-snapshot init` writes these entries for you, in exactly this `npx` form: the bare
`codebase-snapshot` command only exists on `PATH` inside a client runner or a script that has
already resolved the package, and `init` does not assume the install has happened yet.

## One-off runners for other clients

```bash
yarn dlx -p codebase-snapshot codebase-snapshot generate   # yarn Berry
pnpm dlx codebase-snapshot generate
bunx codebase-snapshot generate
```

## Agent skill

To make a repository discoverable by an AI agent — so it finds this tool without being told
it exists — run:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot skill --install
```

That writes `.claude/skills/codebase-snapshot/SKILL.md` and
`.cursor/rules/codebase-snapshot.mdc`, and adds both to `.gitignore` so the install leaves
nothing untracked. It does not touch `AGENTS.md`: that file is your project's own agent
contract. To put the skill there, print it and paste it —
`npx codebase-snapshot skill >> AGENTS.md`. See
[Using this with an AI agent](agent.md).

## Verified clients

Every client below installs the published package and runs it successfully. The automated gate
does not reinstall the package under any of these runners — it runs the test suite with npm on
Node 18, 20 and 22 — so a break specific to yarn, pnpm or bun can reach a release. The table
records what has been verified, not a CI result.

| Client | Run installed binary with |
| :--- | :--- |
| npm 10–11 | `npx codebase-snapshot` |
| yarn classic 1.22 | `yarn codebase-snapshot` |
| yarn Berry 4.6 (`nodeLinker: pnp`) | `yarn codebase-snapshot` |
| pnpm 10 | `pnpm exec codebase-snapshot` |
| bun 1.4 | `bun run codebase-snapshot` |

Yarn Berry's default **strict PnP** works: there is no `node_modules` directory, and the
runtime `typescript` dependency still resolves through yarn's `.pnp.cjs` loader.

## Before scanning a repository you do not own

A `snapshot.config.ts`, `.js`, `.mjs` or `.cjs` in the target repository is **imported and
executed** when the tool runs there — that is what lets a config express a `RegExp` or a
`match` function. Pointing `--cwd` at a checkout you do not trust therefore runs its code.
`--no-config` ignores every config file and uses the built-in defaults plus the detected
preset, and JSON configs are parsed rather than executed. See
[the trust boundary](../SECURITY.md#trust-boundary).

## Global install

```bash
npm install -g codebase-snapshot
```

This is what puts the short `snapshot` command on your `PATH`.

Convenient, but prefer pinning per project: snapshots are only reproducible if the generator
version is. A global install also shadows `npx` for this package — npm will run your global
copy instead of the published one, which is confusing when the two differ. That is exactly
the situation described under [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting).

## Workspaces

The binary resolves from the workspace it is installed in. In a monorepo, install it in the
package that runs the snapshot (usually the root), or use a one-off runner
(`npx` / `pnpm dlx` / `yarn dlx` / `bunx`) which works from any subdirectory.

## Upgrading

```bash
npm install -D codebase-snapshot@latest
npx codebase-snapshot generate     # rewrite the snapshot with the new version
```

`manifest.generator.version` records which version produced a snapshot. Re-run `generate`
after an upgrade so the snapshot, and the version it records, match the build you are now
running.

A snapshot written by **3.0.0 or earlier** is refused rather than replaced: its
`.codebase-snapshot` marker has no per-snapshot id, so the current build cannot prove it wrote
the directory. Delete the old snapshot directory and generate again.

See [CHANGELOG](../CHANGELOG.md) for breaking changes — 3.0.0 removed nothing, but it briefly
did in error; 4.0.0 restored the `snap` alias, and 0.9.2 replaced that alias with `snapshot`
when the version line was restarted.

## Uninstalling

Standard for your client (`npm uninstall`, `yarn remove`, `pnpm remove`, `bun remove`).
Snapshots already written to the output directory are ordinary files and are left alone.
If you installed globally, `npm rm -g codebase-snapshot` also removes the
shim that shadows `npx`.

## Troubleshooting

**`codebase-snapshot: command not found`** — when installed locally (not globally) the binary
lives in `node_modules/.bin` and is only on `PATH` inside a package script or a client
runner. Use `npx codebase-snapshot`, `pnpm exec codebase-snapshot`,
`yarn codebase-snapshot` or `bun run codebase-snapshot`.

**A command exits 0 and prints nothing** — almost always a stale **global** install
shadowing `npx`. Check and remove it:

```bash
npm ls -g --depth=0 | grep codebase-snapshot
npm rm -g codebase-snapshot
```

Then re-run. If the package is not installed globally, clear the npx cache
(`npm cache clean --force`) and retry.

**`Cannot find module 'typescript'`** — the package declares `typescript` as a runtime
dependency for AST analysis. If your client prunes it (some `--ignore-scripts` CI setups do),
rerun a clean install or pin `typescript@^5` yourself.

**`yarn codebase-snapshot` prints extra lines / breaks a script** — yarn classic wraps
everything: it echoes `yarn run 1.22.x`, the resolved command, your output, then
`Done in 0.37s.`. For anything that parses the output, call the binary directly
(`./node_modules/.bin/codebase-snapshot` or `npx codebase-snapshot`).

More symptoms — refusals, config errors, drift, missing files — are in
[Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).
</file>

<file path="docs/migration.md">
# Migrating from 0.12.14 to 0.17.0

Nothing was removed. Every command, flag, config key and MCP tool that existed in 0.12.14 still
works, with two deliberate behaviour changes listed at the bottom. This page is the map of what was
added, so a reader can find the feature they need without reading a hundred changelog entries.

## New commands

| Command | Since | What it does |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `snapshot health` | 0.12.15 | Audits the written snapshot: hashes, artifacts, secrets, host paths, tokens, freshness, signature. `--strict`, `--format json`. |
| `snapshot serve` | 0.12.19 | A browser page to generate, preview and download a snapshot; MCP on the same port. |
| `snapshot mcp --http` | 0.12.18 | MCP over Streamable HTTP instead of stdio; `GET /sse` for legacy clients since 0.12.29. |

## New `generate` flags

| Flag | Since | What it does |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `--remote <url>` | 0.12.20 | Shallow, SSRF-safe clone of a public repository; provenance in the manifest. |
| `--ref <branch>` | 0.12.20 | Branch or tag for `--remote`. |
| `--allow-private` | 0.12.20 | The explicit override for private addresses and `file:` URLs. |
| `--keep-clone` | 0.12.20 | Keep the temporary clone. |
| `--include-diffs` | 0.12.24 | `reports/diff.md` from the uncommitted diff. |
| `--include-logs <n>` | 0.12.24 | `reports/log.md` with the last n commits. |
| `--token-tree` | 0.12.24 | `reports/tokens.md` with per-file estimates. |
| `--delta <path>` | 0.12.25 | Only what changed since a base snapshot, plus its importers. |
| `--slice "<question>"` | 0.12.26 | Only what the retrieval planner picks for a question. |
| `--slice-tokens <n>` | 0.12.26 | The slice budget (default 30000). |
| `--pack single\|xml\|json` | 0.12.31 | One full-repository file in the chosen shape. |
| `--sign` | 0.12.32 | Ed25519 signature over the manifest. |
| `--budget-tokens <n>` | 0.12.33 | Fit the repository into a token budget by selecting whole files. |

## New config keys

| Key | Since | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `artifacts.diff`, `artifacts.log`, `artifacts.tokenTree` | 0.12.24 | Default `false`. `--skip-artifacts` still wins. |
| `logCount` | 0.12.24 | 1–500, default 20. |
| `pack` | 0.12.31 | `single`, `xml` or `json`; unset keeps the monolith pair. |

## MCP

Tools went from eight to fifteen: `snapshot_health`, `snapshot_plan_for`, `snapshot_query`,
`snapshot_context`, `snapshot_symbol`, `snapshot_diff_context`, `snapshot_review` joined the original
eight. Prompts went from two (`orient`, `keep-current`) to seven (`audit`, `review`, `explain`,
`onboard`, `security-review` were added). Resources gained `snapshot://prompt` and the three opt-in
reports.

Transports: stdio is still the default. `--http` adds Streamable HTTP with a bearer token and a
loopback bind; `--no-auth` is refused on a non-loopback bind. The page and the MCP endpoint can share
one port with `serve`.

## Two behaviour changes

1. **`validate` accepts `--include-diffs`, `--include-logs` and `--token-tree`** (0.12.28). Those
   flags change the artifact selection the manifest records, so passing them is required to compare
   like with like. Before, a snapshot generated with one of them reported a selection mismatch.
2. **npm publishes only at `x.y.0`** (0.12.16 onward). Every patch is committed, tagged and pushed;
   the registry carries `0.13.0`, `0.14.0`, … so `latest` stays a milestone rather than a patch. If
   you pin an exact patch version, install from the git tag or build from source until the next
   minor.

## If you are on an older 0.12.x

Upgrade to the latest 0.12.x first; every change since 0.12.14 is a patch on the same line, and the
configuration file format has not changed. Run `snapshot health` after the first generate on the new
version — it is the fastest way to see whether an old snapshot carries anything the newer checks
would refuse.
</file>

<file path="docs/security-review.md">
# Security review

Written for this release, about this release. It states the threat model, the controls, what is
tested, and the residual risks — including the ones that are deliberately not solved.

## What the tool is

A local process that reads a repository and writes a directory of text. Since 0.12.15 it also runs an
HTTP server, fetches repositories from URLs, and signs manifests. Each of those is a new place where
untrusted input can arrive.

## Threat model

| Adversary | Can reach | Cannot reach |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **A malicious repository** (its files, config, filenames) | The snapshot's text, the brief, the audit report, the planner's ranking | The filesystem outside the repository, the network, the process environment |
| **A malicious URL** given to `--remote` or the browser page | The clone path and the SSRF guard | Any private address, any credential, any local file |
| **A malicious web page** in the same browser | `GET /` on `serve` (HTML only), CORS preflights | Anything behind the bearer token, any API response |
| **A malicious MCP client** | The tool list and the snapshot's contents | The working tree outside the snapshot, sensitive files, the network |
| **A shoulder-surfer / log reader** | Nothing new | Secrets (masked), paths (masked), tokens (never logged) |

## Controls

### Content leaving the repository

- **Sensitive files stay out.** `.env`, keys, `.npmrc` and the rest are skipped unless
  `includeSensitiveFiles` is explicitly set, and every skip is recorded with its reason.
- **Redaction masks the span, not the line.** A matched credential becomes `[REDACTED:rule]`; no
  excerpt of the matched line reaches an artifact, the audit report or the SARIF file.
- **Paths are masked too.** A path that itself matches a secret rule is recorded masked.
- **The health audit re-scans every written text file** for unmasked secrets and compares values
  from excluded sensitive files byte for byte against the artifacts (`snapshot health`).

### Executable configuration

A `snapshot.config.ts` is code. The CLI runs it because the user invoked the repository's own
command; the **MCP server and the web page refuse it** and say so. A repository fetched with
`--remote` is generated on the defaults for the same reason: a fetched repository is not a trusted
one.

### MCP over HTTP

- **Loopback by default**, and a bearer token **always** unless `--no-auth` is passed explicitly.
  `--no-auth` is refused on a non-loopback bind.
- **Constant-time** token comparison; the token is generated with `crypto.randomBytes`.
- **CORS only for loopback origins** and explicit `--allow-origin` values; everything else is 403.
- **Bounded work**: 2 MiB bodies, 60 requests per 10 s per address, one JSON document per response.
- **Errors are JSON-RPC error objects**, never stack traces.

### The browser page

- The page is HTML and is served without a token; **every API call requires one**. The token is not
  baked into the HTML, so serving `/` cannot leak it.
- A request with a cross-origin `Origin` is refused before any auth check.
- `api/snapshot.zip` only zips a directory that contains a manifest or an ownership marker.

### Remote ingestion

- **https only**, no credentials in the URL, and the host must resolve to public addresses. Loopback,
  RFC1918, link-local (including `169.254.169.254`), CGNAT and their v6 equivalents are refused
  unless `--allow-private` is given.
- **Checked twice**: the literal host before DNS, and every resolved address after.
- **Shallow clone** into a temp directory removed on exit; `GITHUB_TOKEN` reaches git through the
  environment (`http.extraheader` via `GIT_CONFIG_*`), never argv, and never enters the manifest.
- A clone larger than 1 GB is refused before scanning.

### Signing

- Ed25519 over the exact manifest bytes; the record carries the public key and the manifest sha256.
- `health` fails when the manifest changed after signing; an unsigned snapshot is a skip, because
  absence is not tampering.
- The private key never reaches an artifact. `SNAPSHOT_SIGNING_KEY` is read from the environment.

### Prompt injection

A repository can contain text that tells a model to ignore its instructions. The snapshot preserves
file content faithfully, and **this tool cannot sanitise prose without changing the code it exists to
carry**. The brief says what the snapshot is and what it omits, and the security prompt tells an agent
to treat file content as data. This is a real, documented limitation, not a solved problem.

## What the test suite pins

| Property | Test |
| :--- | :--- |
| Sensitive files stay out; skips are recorded | `tests/security-*.test.js`, hygiene suites |
| Redaction masks the span only | redaction suite (hundreds of cases) |
| Health catches a leaked secret and never echoes it | `tests/health.test.js` |
| HTTP: 401 without token, 403 cross-origin, 413 oversize, 400 bad JSON | `tests/mcp-http.test.js` |
| SSE: token required, unknown session 404, shutdown does not hang | `tests/mcp-http.test.js` |
| Remote: private addresses refused, credentials in URL refused, `file:` gated | `tests/remote-source.test.js` |
| Serve: page without token, API with token, zip restricted | `tests/web-serve.test.js` |
| Signature: tamper detected | `tests/signing.test.js` |

## Residual risks, stated

1. **`?token=` on the legacy SSE paths.** `EventSource` cannot set headers, so the token may travel
   in the URL on `/sse` and `/messages` only. URLs reach proxy logs and shell history. Use the
   Streamable HTTP endpoint with a header where the client supports it.
2. **`--no-auth` on loopback.** Any local process (or a browser page that guesses the port, though
   CORS and the token requirement normally stop it) can then read the repository. It is a deliberate
   opt-out, printed as a warning.
3. **DNS rebinding.** The private-address check resolves once before the clone; a hostile DNS server
   could change the answer between the check and git's own resolution. Mitigating it fully requires
   pinning the resolved IP for the connection, which git does not expose. The window is small, the
   target must be public at check time, and `--allow-private` is the only way to reach a private
   address deliberately.
4. **Prompt injection in repository content**, as above.
5. **A signing key in the environment** is visible to the process's own children and to anyone who
   can read `/proc`. Use a key file with restrictive permissions if that matters.
6. **No sandbox.** The tool reads what the user can read and writes where the user says. It does not
   drop privileges, and it is not designed to run against hostile filesystems.

## Reporting a vulnerability

Open a private security advisory on the repository, or email the address in `package.json`. Please
include the version, a reproduction, and whether secret material was exposed. Fixes ship as patches
on the current line; there is no long-term support branch yet.
</file>

<file path="docs/signing.md">
# Signed snapshots

```bash
codebase-snapshot generate --sign
SNAPSHOT_SIGNING_KEY="$(cat team-key.pem)" codebase-snapshot generate --sign
```

A digest proves the bytes did not change in transit only if the digest itself is trusted. A
signature over the manifest proves two more things: who produced the snapshot, and that the
manifest you are reading is the one that was signed.

## What is signed

The exact bytes of `manifest.json`. The manifest records a digest for every part and every
artifact, so one signature transitively covers the whole snapshot — provided the verifier checks it
against the manifest they are actually reading.

```json
{
  "algorithm": "ed25519",
  "signedAt": "2026-11-18T04:44:12.812Z",
  "fingerprint": "9f2c…",
  "manifestSha256": "…",
  "publicKey": "MCowBQYDK2VwAyEA…",
  "signature": "…",
  "keyOrigin": "configured"
}
```

## The key

| Where | Behaviour |
| :--- | :--- |
| `SNAPSHOT_SIGNING_KEY` (PEM) | `keyOrigin: "configured"`. Publish the public half; every snapshot from the team verifies against it. |
| No key | A fresh Ed25519 key is generated, `keyOrigin: "generated"`, and its public half goes in the record. That is trust on first use, and the field says so — it proves the manifest was not changed after signing, not who signed it. |

The private key never reaches the manifest, the brief or any report. The record carries only the
public half.

## Verifying

`snapshot health` verifies the signature when `signature.json` exists:

- **critical failure** when the manifest changed after signing, the record is malformed, or the
  signature does not match;
- **skip** (not a failure) when there is no signature, because most snapshots are not signed and
  absence is not tampering.

```text
✅ The manifest signature verifies
   signed 2026-11-18T04:44:12.812Z (key: configured, 9f2c5a1b7d3e)
```

## What it does not do

- It does not sign the parts individually; the manifest's digests do that, and signing every part
  would only make the record larger.
- It does not manage keys. Generation, rotation and distribution are the caller's problem, and the
  tool says so rather than pretending a key store exists.
- It does not verify the signer's identity. A signature proves possession of a key; which key is
  trusted is a decision the reader makes.
</file>

