# Using this with an AI agent

The package ships two things that make it usable by an agent that has never heard of it: a
**machine-readable contract** and a **bundled skill**.

Nothing needs to be installed globally and no prior knowledge of the tool is required.

## The three commands an agent needs

```bash
# 1. Read the contract. This is the machine-readable replacement for --help.
npx codebase-snapshot describe --format json

# 2. Write a commented config that lists every setting (non-interactive).
npx codebase-snapshot init --yes

# 3. Generate and verify.
npx codebase-snapshot generate
npx codebase-snapshot validate
```

The bare package name works; pin an exact version (`npx codebase-snapshot --version` prints the current one) when the same version must run on every
machine.

### One command instead of three

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot --autopilot
```

Sets the project up — config, npm scripts, devDependency, `.gitignore`, install — and generates,
with no prompts. It keeps an existing config, and `--no-install` avoids touching the lockfile.
`setup` is the same operation as a command word.

### Do not rely on the bare command

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot          # ❌ in an unconfigured project this exits 2
```

An agent has no terminal, so the guided first run cannot ask its question and refuses instead of
generating with settings nobody chose. That exit `2` is the tool being honest, not broken: say
which setup you want — `--autopilot`, `init --yes`, or `generate` on the documented defaults.

## The contract

`describe --format json` returns the whole tool as data on stdout:

| Field | Contents |
| :--- | :--- |
| `invocation` | The supported one-shot and installed commands, and the bin names. |
| `workflow` | An ordered six-step procedure: verify → understand → configure → generate → verify output → consume. |
| `commands` | Every command with what it does, its positionals and its exit codes. |
| `options` | Every option with its type, default, which commands it applies to, and a summary. |
| `exitCodes` | `0` success, `1` drift or refused generation, `2` usage error. |
| `presets` | All 25, with priority, recommended budget and ecosystem ignores. |
| `artifacts` | Each artifact with its on/off default and what it contains. |
| `config` | Every config filename and every setting with its default. |
| `output` | The directory layout and how to read `manifest.json`. |
| `safety` | What is excluded or redacted, and which directories are refused. |
| `troubleshooting` | Symptom → cause → fix. |

It is generated from the same code the CLI runs — presets, artifact names and versions are
read directly — and a test compares its documented defaults against a real
`generate --dry-run` manifest. If a default changes, the test fails rather than the contract
silently misleading an agent.

`describe` with no `--format` renders the same content for a human.

## The skill

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot skill            # print it (stdout)
npx codebase-snapshot skill --install  # write it into the repository
```

Printing goes to stdout deliberately, so it can be redirected:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot skill > SKILL.md
```

`--install` writes the same skill into the registries agent tools read by name:

| Target | Path | Read by |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `claude` | `.claude/skills/codebase-snapshot/SKILL.md` | Claude Code auto-discovery |
| `cursor` | `.cursor/rules/codebase-snapshot.mdc` | Cursor rules |

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot skill --install --target claude   # one registry
npx codebase-snapshot skill --install                   # both (default)
```

Both are **registries keyed by skill name**: the path carries `codebase-snapshot`, so the file
cannot be mistaken for your project's own documentation, and two skills cannot collide.

**It never writes `AGENTS.md`.** That file is the repository's own agent contract — build
commands, conventions, house rules — and an install that creates or edits it puts a tool's
marketing where the project's rules belong. If you want the skill in there, print it and paste
it, so the diff and the commit are yours:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot skill >> AGENTS.md
```

The install is **idempotent**, and whatever it *creates* is appended to `.gitignore` in the
same run — these are a tool's files written into your repository, and `git status` should not
turn that into a decision about what to commit. A file that already existed is never ignored.
`--format json` reports what was created, updated or unchanged, and which ignore entries were
added.

`codebase-snapshot init` does none of this: it writes the config, the npm scripts, the
devDependency and the `.gitignore` entries for the snapshot directory, and nothing else.

## When the client speaks MCP

`codebase-snapshot mcp` serves the repository to a Model Context Protocol client over stdio: eleven
tools, `snapshot://` resources and two prompts. Prefer it over shelling out when the agent already
has an MCP connection, for two reasons that are properties of the design rather than of the wiring:

- **Reads come from the snapshot, not the working tree.** `snapshot_read` extracts a file's section
  from the emitted part, so a file the snapshot excluded cannot be read back and a redacted value
  stays redacted.
- **Staleness is a first-class question.** `snapshot_validate` answers "has the code moved since
  this was built", which an agent with only a filesystem tool has no way to ask.

The tools are listed in [the MCP guide](mcp.md) and in the `mcp` block of
`describe --format json`. A `root` argument may name a sub-directory of the directory the server was
started for, and nothing outside it.

## Why both a contract and a skill

The contract is for **doing** — the data an agent needs to pick correct commands and options.
The skill is for **knowing when to act** — it carries the trigger conditions, the ordered
workflow, and the surprises an agent cannot infer from a schema: sensitive files excluded by
default, secrets redacted, the output directory being tool-owned.

## What an agent should avoid

- **Do not point `--out-dir` at source code.** It is refused, and the refusal is deliberate.
- **Do not add `--include-sensitive-files`** unless the user explicitly asked for credential
  files; by default they are excluded and recorded as `sensitive-file` in `manifest.skipped[]`.
- **Do not work around redaction.** If a value is masked, ask the user rather than reading the
  original file.
- **Do not assume exit 2 means the repository is broken.** It means the command was wrong.
  Re-read the contract.
- **Do not treat a bare `npx` that prints nothing as a repository problem.** A stale global
  install shadows `npx`; see [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#the-command-does-nothing).

## Verifying an agent can drive it

`tests/agent-simulation.test.js` runs the published instructions as an agent would: read
`--help`, discover `describe`, then execute the six workflow steps in order against an 8-file
Django fixture.

It asserts what the contract promises at each step: `--version` prints a version, every
published option states where it applies, `init --yes` writes a config naming **every**
setting the contract lists, `generate --format json` exits `0` with
`validation.status: "passed"` and the preset `python-django`, every fixture file is either in
`manifest.files[]` or explained in `manifest.skipped[]`, each `files[]` entry carries its
classification and hash, and `validate` then exits `0`. If an agent following the published
words would fail, that test fails.

When you parse the manifest, remember what is conditional: `manifest.files[]` carries
`relativePath`, `layer`, `kind`, `language`, `part`, `inclusionMode` and `hashSha256` for
every included file, but `summary` and `summaryQuality` only for a language the tool can
analyse. A `.gitignore` or plain-text entry has neither key, and a file whose summary was too
large for its part carries `summaryOmitted: true` (with `summaryOmittedReason`) instead of a
`summary`. `manifest.incomplete[]` is the list of files whose body is not the whole file, with
`truncationReason` and `omittedRanges`; `files[]` carries the same two fields. `encoding` appears only
when a file was not plain UTF-8: `utf-16le`/`utf-16be` were transcoded, and `utf-8-lossy` means
the body you are reading contains `U+FFFD` where undecodable bytes were.
</file>

<file path="docs/budget.md">
# Fit a repository into a token budget

```bash
codebase-snapshot generate --budget-tokens 100000
```

A part budget truncates files to fit; a **token budget** selects whole files to fit. The difference
matters: a torn file gives a reader half a function, while a selected set gives them fewer
subsystems that are each complete.

## How it chooses

Every scanned file is scored once:

| Signal | Weight | Why |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Entry point (`main`, `index`, `app`, `server`, `cli`, `package.json`, `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml`, `pyproject.toml`) | +100 | Without it, nothing else makes sense. |
| Configuration layer | +40 | What the project is, before what it does. |
| Source layer | +20 | The code. |
| Test layer | −10 | Useful, but after the code it tests. |
| Importer count | up to +60, 6 per importer | A file many others import is load-bearing. |
| Cost | up to −40 | A cheap file that matters beats an expensive one that barely does. |

Files are taken in score order while the running token estimate fits; a file that would exceed the
budget is skipped whole, not cut. The estimate is one token per four bytes of emitted body — the
same estimate the manifest reports.

## What it records

```json
"budget": {
  "maxTokens": 100000,
  "usedTokens": 96812,
  "files": ["package.json", "src/main.ts", "src/core.ts", "..."],
  "dropped": 418,
  "coverage": {
    "byLayer": { "config": { "kept": 3, "total": 3 }, "src": { "kept": 12, "total": 40 } },
    "entryPoints": { "kept": 2, "total": 2 },
    "symbols": { "kept": 18, "total": 61 }
  }
}
```

`coverage` is the honest half of the report: which layers kept what, how many entry points
survived, and how many exported symbols made it in. When an entry point is lost the run warns —
a snapshot without its entry point is a tour without a front door.

Every scanned path that did not fit is in `skipped[]` with reason `not-selected` and the detail
"did not fit the --budget-tokens budget". The brief states the budget, the selection size and the
drop count, so a reader knows what they are missing.

## When to use it

- "I have 128k of context and this repository is 2M tokens": this is the one-command answer.
- A first pass over an unfamiliar repository, before deciding what to read next.
- Pair it with `--delta` to spend the budget only on what changed.

It is not a replacement for `--slice`: a slice answers a question, a budget answers a limit, and
the CLI refuses both at once rather than guessing which narrowing you meant.
</file>

<file path="docs/how-to-context-budget.md">
# How to budget LLM context files for a codebase

Context is a budget, not a bin. Every token spent on a lockfile is a token not spent on the function
being asked about, and the failure is silent — the model simply gets vaguer. This page is about
choosing the numbers and knowing what they cost.

## Start from the destination, not the repository

Work backwards. The constraint is whatever consumes the output:

| Consumer | What actually limits you |
| :--- | :--- |
| A chat window | The window, minus room for the conversation and the answer |
| A RAG pipeline | Embedding chunk size, and how many chunks get retrieved per query |
| An agent loop | The window, minus tool schemas and minus the reasoning it has left to do |
| A review artifact | Human attention — usually far smaller than any window |

An agent needs room to *think*, so it wants the smallest context that answers the question. A chat
window can take the largest.

## The numbers that matter

- **`maxPartBytes`** — the hard ceiling for one part. This is a promise, not an average: no part
  exceeds it. Set it to the destination's limit and stop worrying about the largest file.
- **`largeFileBytes`** — above this, a file becomes a head/tail excerpt rather than its full body.
  It defaults to `maxPartBytes` and may not be set above it.
  This is what stops one generated 40 MB file from consuming an entire snapshot.
- **`budgets.byLayer`** — different ceilings per layer. Documentation is usually worth less per byte
  than source; `{ docs: 40_000 }` is a common shape.
- **`maxMonolithBytes`** — the single-file artifact roughly doubles output, so it is off by default
  and capped when on.

```js
const config = {
  maxPartBytes: "120k",
  largeFileBytes: "120k",   // defaults to maxPartBytes; may not exceed it
  budgets: { default: "120k", byLayer: { docs: 40_000, config: 20_000 } },
  artifacts: { monolith: false },
};
export default config;
```

## What each byte buys

Measured on this repository's own snapshot, as a rough guide:

| Layer | Share of bytes | Worth it? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Source | largest | yes — this is the question |
| Docs and comments | large | partly; often the best value per byte, but not when duplicated |
| Config | small | yes — it explains how the source runs |
| Tests | often 30–40% | rarely, for a code question |
| Generated and vendored | varies | almost never |

The cheapest large win in most repositories is excluding tests and build output. The second cheapest
is excluding duplicated documentation (the same content in a wiki, a `docs/` tree and a README).

## Do not trust a size estimate you have not measured

A snapshot is auditable: `manifest.json` records `includedBytes` and `includedLines` **measured from
the block actually emitted**, not from the file on disk. That distinction matters — a truncated file
does not report its on-disk size.

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot report
```

`report` prints totals, per-layer metrics and the secret audit, so you can see where the budget went
before you spend it on a model call.

## Tuning loop

1. Generate with a ceiling that matches the destination.
2. Run `report`; look at the per-layer breakdown.
3. Cut the largest layer that does not answer your question.
4. Re-generate and compare `manifest.totals.emittedLines` and `manifest.counts.partsCount`.
5. Keep the settings that produced a snapshot you actually used — the config is the durable artifact,
   not the output.

## The failure this prevents

Without a ceiling, the boundary is wherever the tool stopped. Two runs can cut differently, so a
worse answer is indistinguishable from a different cut. With `maxPartBytes` fixed and output
deterministic, a changed answer means changed code — which is the only interpretation that lets you
debug it.

## Next

- [Configuration](configuration.md) — every setting, all 25 presets
- [Snapshot output](output.md) — the full `manifest.json` schema
- [How to feed a codebase to an LLM](how-to-feed-a-repo-to-claude.md)
</file>

<file path="docs/stale-context-is-your-bug.md">
# Stale LLM context is a bug

A snapshot is a copy. The code keeps moving. Nothing in the copy says so, and nothing in the
model can notice.

That is the whole failure mode: you hand a model a faithful description of your repository on
Monday, change four files on Wednesday, and on Thursday ask about a function you have already
deleted. The answer is confident, plausible and about a repository that no longer exists.

```text
you ──▶ snapshot (Monday) ──▶ model ──▶ answer about Monday's code
             code moves (Wednesday) ────┘
```

There is no error message, because nothing failed. The model did exactly what it was given.

## Why "just regenerate it" is not a control

Regenerating is cheap — it takes a second. The problem is that you have to *know* to do it, and
nobody notices a stale snapshot until an answer is wrong. A habit is not a guard, and a README
line that says "re-run this before asking" is a habit.

What you need is not more discipline. It is a comparison that can fail.

## Three things that make drift visible

### 1. An identity for the copy

Every run writes `manifest.fingerprint`, a hash of what was included — derived from the content
of every file and from the settings that shaped the output. Two repositories that differ by one
byte have different fingerprints, and so do two runs of the same tree with a different budget;
two runs over the same tree with the same settings, on different machines, have the same one.

```bash
codebase-snapshot report --format json | jq -r .manifest.fingerprint
```

Quote that value wherever the snapshot is used — in the prompt, in the PR description, in the
ticket. It turns "the snapshot we sent" into a value that can be compared later.

### 2. A comparison you can run

`validate` re-scans the repository, re-hashes each file the snapshot claims to contain, and
compares. It exits `1` when the code has moved, and `0` when the snapshot still describes what
is on disk.

```bash
codebase-snapshot validate
```

```text
❌ Snapshot validation failed:
  - 1 file(s) have been modified since the snapshot was generated.
  - modified: src/parser/partition.ts
  - new file: src/presets/evidence.ts
  - deleted: src/legacy/old-partitioner.ts
```

The first line is the count; the lines after it name each path. `new file` is a file on disk
the snapshot does not contain, and `deleted` is the opposite.

That is the difference between a copy and an artefact. A copy is trusted; an artefact is
checked.

### 3. A record of what was left out

`manifest.skipped[]` names every path the snapshot did not include, with a reason for each:

| Reason | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| `gitignore` | the repository's own rules excluded it |
| `default-ignore` | `node_modules`, build output, VCS metadata |
| `sensitive-file` | `.env`, `*.pem`, `.npmrc` — excluded unless explicitly requested |
| `binary` | not text, so not useful as context |
| `outside-root` | a symlink pointing out of the repository |
| `nested-repository` | a submodule or nested checkout |
| `read-error` | it could not be read, and the run says so instead of dropping it quietly |

When an answer is wrong because the model never saw a file, this is the list that tells you
whether the file was excluded on purpose or by accident.

The second half of the same question is a file that was included but not in full.
`manifest.incomplete[]` names every one of those with its `reason` — `large-file-budget`,
`part-budget` or `section-metadata` — and `omittedRanges`, the 1-based source lines that are
missing or partial. A server that only reports what it included is telling half the story; this
is the half that explains a confident answer about code the model never saw.

## Gate it

The comparison is only a guard if something runs it. In CI, after whatever changes the tree:

```yaml
- name: Snapshot is still current
  run: npx codebase-snapshot validate
```

A failure means the context you are about to rely on describes a different repository. Whether
that blocks the pipeline or just warns is a policy question; having it at all is not.

The same gate works for a long-running agent: run `validate` before a session that will read
the snapshot, and regenerate when it fails.

## What this does not tell you

Worth being precise about the limits, because "validate" sounds like more than it is:

- **It compares files, not meaning.** A refactor that preserves every byte proves nothing is
  wrong — correctly, because nothing is.
- **It cannot see what the model did with the text.** A current snapshot can still be
  misunderstood, summarised badly, or ignored.
- **It says nothing about whether the snapshot was a good selection.** Use `explain <path>` to
  see why one path was included or skipped, and `report` for the shape of the whole run.
- **It does not follow history.** If you keep three snapshots from three branches, each one
  validates against the branch it was generated from.

## When this is overkill

If you generate a snapshot, paste it into a chat, and throw it away in the same hour, you do not
need any of this — `snapshot` alone is the right amount of ceremony.

The controls start to pay for themselves the moment a snapshot outlives the session that made
it: a checked-in context directory, a scheduled job, a review bot, an agent that runs for days,
or a team where the person who generated the snapshot is not the person who reads it.

## Making the gate automatic

Validate only helps if it runs. To gate commits on it:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot init --install-hook     # or: setup --install-hook
```

That writes `.git/hooks/pre-commit`, which refuses a commit while the snapshot is out of date
and tells the person committing what to run. It is deliberately cautious about an existing hook:
a `pre-commit` this tool did not write is left byte-for-byte alone, and the output says which
lines to add instead. Remove the file to stop the check.

## Related

- [Budgeting LLM context](how-to-context-budget.md) — choosing a part size that fits the window
- [Snapshot output](output.md) — the manifest fields this page refers to
- [CLI reference](cli.md#validate--check) — `validate` in full
</file>

<file path="docs/vs-code2prompt.md">
# codebase-snapshot vs code2prompt

`code2prompt` is a Rust CLI that renders a codebase through a Handlebars template into a prompt. The
templating is the feature — it decides the shape of what you get. This tool decides the *size and
the freshness* of what you get, and leaves the shape to a manifest. They are close to opposites,
which makes choosing between them straightforward.

> Written by the author of `codebase-snapshot`. code2prompt is a separate project; the summary
> reflects its commonly documented behaviour at the time of writing rather than a benchmark.

## The short version

**code2prompt** — you write a template, it renders your repository into it. Output shape is entirely
yours.

**codebase-snapshot** — output shape is fixed and predictable: budgeted Markdown parts, plus a JSON
manifest with hashes. You tune what goes in, not what the page looks like.

If the prompt format is the thing you care about: **use code2prompt.**

## Side by side

| | codebase-snapshot | code2prompt |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Output shape | Fixed: parts + `manifest.json` + reports | **Yours** — Handlebars template |
| Custom prompt scaffolding | No — that is out of scope | **Yes**, the core feature |
| Hard size ceiling | **Yes** — `maxPartBytes` | Not to this project's knowledge |
| Staleness check | `validate` exits non-zero on drift | Not a feature |
| Reproducible output | Byte-identical parts, with a fingerprint | Template-driven; no determinism guarantee |
| Distribution | Node.js, npm | Single Rust binary, no runtime |
| Machine index | `manifest.json` with hashes and skip reasons | Template output only |
| Language awareness | 25 presets, AST/structural/heuristic summaries | Template access to file contents |
| Secret handling | 32 defaults, value-span masking, audit table | Ignore rules |

## Where code2prompt is the better choice

- **You need a specific prompt structure** — a system preamble, per-file headers, a task framing.
  Templates are the right abstraction and this tool has no answer to them.
- **You want a single binary with no runtime.** Rust means no Node install on the target machine.
- **You are generating prompts, not context artifacts.** If the output is a prompt, you want a
  templating engine.

## Where this is the better choice

- **You need the output to fit a budget.** `maxPartBytes` is a ceiling: a file that cannot fit one
  part becomes a head/tail preview with an omission marker, listed in `incomplete[]` with the line
  ranges it lost, rather than silently blowing the limit.
- **You need to detect staleness.** `validate` compares every file hash plus a digest of the
  generation settings, so a changed budget counts as drift. Prompt generators generally have no
  concept of a snapshot that can expire.
- **You need reproducibility.** Frozen output means the artifact can be diffed in review or cached by
  hash — not just regenerated and hoped over.
- **You need to explain exclusions.** `manifest.skipped[]` names a reason for every path left out;
  `explain <path>` answers for one.
- **You need an agent to run it unaided.** `describe --format json` is the whole contract as data.

## Both at once

Legitimate and common: generate the parts with this tool, then reference those parts from a template
you render yourself. The two are not competing for the same slot — one produces a payload, the other
produces a prompt that might contain it.

## Honest summary

If your problem is *"my prompt is not structured the way I want"*, code2prompt solves it and this
tool does not. If your problem is *"my context does not fit, and I cannot tell whether it is still
accurate"*, that is this tool's entire reason to exist.
</file>

<file path="scripts/check-site-links.mjs">
/**
 * Fails when a rendered page links to something the site does not contain.
 *
 * Two things are checked: that the target file exists, and that a link's `#fragment` names an
 * element on the page it targets. The second check is the one this file shipped without, and the
 * reason every table-of-contents link on the published site was dead: the pages had heading text
 * but no heading ids, and an existence-only check cannot see the difference between a heading and
 * a paragraph. A fragment is checked against the id set of the page it lands on, so it covers both
 * an in-page `href="#commands"` and a cross-page `href="configuration.html#project-presets"`.
 *
 * Runs against the built site, so run `npm run site` first. `npm run verify` runs this script
 * after the build and before the audit, so a pull request catches a dead link; the deploy
 * workflow runs it directly before uploading the artifact.
 */
import * as fs from "node:fs";
import * as path from "node:path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";

const ROOT = path.join(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), "..");
const SITE = path.join(ROOT, "site");
const write = (...parts) => process.stdout.write(`${parts.join("")}\n`);

/** Decodes a fragment's percent-escapes, leaving malformed input exactly as written. */
function decodeFragment(fragment) {
  try {
    return decodeURIComponent(fragment);
  } catch {
    return fragment;
  }
}

/**
 * Every `#fragment` must resolve against the ids of the page it targets.
 *
 * `pages` maps a page name (`cli.html`) to its HTML, so one rule covers a same-page anchor and
 * a cross-page one. A duplicate id is reported too: only the first can be linked to reliably.
 *
 * Exported so a test can run the check over rendered pages without a built `site/`.
 */
export function findAnchorProblems(pages) {
  const idsByPage = new Map();
  const problems = [];

  for (const [name, html] of pages) {
    const ids = new Set();
    for (const match of html.matchAll(/\bid="([^"]+)"/g)) {
      if (ids.has(match[1])) problems.push(`${name}: duplicate id "${match[1]}"`);
      ids.add(match[1]);
    }
    idsByPage.set(name, ids);
  }

  let checked = 0;
  for (const [name, html] of pages) {
    for (const match of html.matchAll(/href="([^"]*)"/g)) {
      const href = match[1];
      if (/^(?:https?:|mailto:)/.test(href)) continue;
      const hash = href.indexOf("#");
      if (hash < 0) continue;
      const fragment = decodeFragment(href.slice(hash + 1));
      if (!fragment) continue;
      const beforeHash = href.slice(0, hash);
      const target = beforeHash === "" ? name : beforeHash.replace(/^\//, "") || "index.html";
      const targetIds = idsByPage.get(target);
      // A missing target file, or a non-HTML one, is the existence check's job.
      if (targetIds === undefined) continue;
      checked += 1;
      if (!targetIds.has(fragment)) {
        problems.push(`${name} -> ${href} (${target} has no element with id "${fragment}")`);
      }
    }
  }

  return { checked, problems };
}

function checkSite() {
  if (!fs.existsSync(SITE)) {
    write("check-site-links: site/ does not exist — run `npm run site` first");
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const files = new Set(fs.readdirSync(SITE));
  const pages = new Map();
  const problems = [];
  let checked = 0;

  for (const name of files) {
    if (name.endsWith(".html")) pages.set(name, fs.readFileSync(path.join(SITE, name), "utf8"));
  }

  for (const [name, html] of pages) {
    // `src` as well as `href`: the two dead assets on the published site were both `src`
    // attributes, and an existence check that only reads links cannot see them. Fragments are
    // an `href` concept and are handled by `findAnchorProblems` below.
    for (const match of html.matchAll(/(?:href|src)="([^"]+)"/g)) {
      const href = match[1];
      if (/^(?:https?:|mailto:|data:|#)/.test(href)) continue;
      checked += 1;
      // Origin-absolute paths (`/llms.txt`) are served from the site root once
      // deployed, so they resolve against the same directory as relative ones.
      const target = href.split("#")[0].replace(/^\//, "");
      if (!target) continue;
      // Resolve against the site root rather than the top-level listing: `assets/favicon.svg`
      // is a real file one directory down, and a flat `files.has()` reported every icon on every
      // page as broken the moment the site gained an assets directory.
      const resolved = path.resolve(SITE, target);
      // The separator matters: without it a sibling directory whose name merely begins with the
      // site's (`../site-other`) satisfied `startsWith`, so a link escaping the site root was read
      // as inside it.
      if (resolved !== SITE && !resolved.startsWith(SITE + path.sep)) {
        problems.push(`${name} -> ${href} (escapes the site root)`);
        continue;
      }
      if (!fs.existsSync(resolved)) problems.push(`${name} -> ${href}`);
    }
  }

  const anchors = findAnchorProblems(pages);
  problems.push(...anchors.problems);

  // Every sitemap URL must be a page this build actually produced, or the sitemap
  // advertises a 404 to the one crawler we are trying to satisfy.
  //
  // The base path is taken from the canonical on index.html rather than hardcoded,
  // so this also asserts that the sitemap and the canonical agree about where the
  // site lives — a subpath and an origin root are both valid, a mismatch is not.
  const indexHtml = fs.readFileSync(path.join(SITE, "index.html"), "utf8");
  const canonical = indexHtml.match(/rel="canonical" href="([^"]+)"/)?.[1] ?? "";
  const base = canonical.replace(/\/$/, "");
  if (!base) {
    write("check-site-links: index.html has no canonical, so the sitemap base is unknown");
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const sitemap = fs.readFileSync(path.join(SITE, "sitemap.xml"), "utf8");
  for (const match of sitemap.matchAll(/<loc>([^<]+)<\/loc>/g)) {
    const loc = new URL(match[1]);
    const pathname = loc.pathname.replace(/\/$/, "");
    if (!`${loc.origin}${pathname}`.startsWith(base)) {
      problems.push(`sitemap -> ${match[1]} (outside the canonical base ${base})`);
      continue;
    }
    const relativePath = pathname.slice(new URL(base).pathname.replace(/\/$/, "").length).replace(/^\//, "");
    const target = relativePath === "" ? "index.html" : relativePath;
    if (!files.has(target)) problems.push(`sitemap -> ${match[1]} (no ${target})`);
  }

  if (problems.length > 0) {
    for (const problem of problems) write(`FAIL: ${problem}`);
    write(`check-site-links: ${problems.length} problem(s) in ${files.size} file(s)`);
    process.exit(1);
  }

  write(
    `check-site-links: ${files.size} files, ${checked} internal links, ${anchors.checked} anchors, 0 problems`
  );
}

function isInvokedDirectly() {
  const entry = process.argv[1];
  if (!entry) return false;
  return path.resolve(entry) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
}

if (isInvokedDirectly()) checkSite();
</file>

<file path="src/generator/audit.ts">
import type { Manifest, SecretFinding, SecretRule } from "../types.js";
import { findSecretInPath } from "../scanner/files.js";
import { codeCell, escapeControls, formatNumber, inlineCode, tableCell } from "../terminal.js";

/**
 * Builds the security/health report.
 *
 * This report deliberately contains **no source text**. It previously printed a
 * 120-character excerpt of every matched line, so each secret that had just been
 * redacted out of `parts/*.md` was written here in clear text. Paths are masked
 * with the same rules as the manifest: a token in a *filename* is a secret too,
 * and the matched-file table and the large-file table both print paths.
 *
 * Every path printed here is also re-spelled through {@link escapeControls}: the
 * tables are read on a terminal, and a bidi override in a name would reorder the
 * row around it. Escaping happens after masking, so a masked secret is never
 * un-masked by it.
 */
export function generateAuditReport(
  manifest: Manifest,
  secretFindings: SecretFinding[],
  secretRules?: readonly SecretRule[]
): string {
  const mask = (value: string): string =>
    escapeControls(findSecretInPath(value, secretRules)?.masked ?? value);
  // Every table row below puts a repository-supplied name inside inline code and a `|` cell, so a
  // layer called `core|x` or a path containing a backtick used to break the table it was listed in.
  const code = (value: string): string => codeCell(mask(value));
  const lines: string[] = [
    "# Codebase Health & Security Audit",
    "",
    `**Generated:** ${manifest.generatedAt}  `,
    `**Git Branch:** ${inlineCode(escapeControls(manifest.git.branch))} (\`${manifest.git.commit.slice(0, 8)}\`${manifest.git.isDirty ? " - dirty" : ""})  `,
    `**Total Files:** ${formatNumber(manifest.counts.totalFiles)}  `,
    `**Source Lines:** ${formatNumber(manifest.counts.totalLines)}  `,
    `**Emitted Lines:** ${formatNumber(manifest.totals.emittedLines ?? manifest.counts.totalLines)}  `,
    `**Total Size:** ${(manifest.counts.totalSizeBytes / (1024 * 1024)).toFixed(2)} MiB  `,
    // This counts the bytes that were *emitted* after redaction, not the bytes
    // that were masked: calling it "redacted bytes" made a repository with zero
    // secrets report a non-zero redacted-byte figure directly above "no secrets
    // detected". The number of masked lines is reported in section 1 instead.
    `**Emitted Content Bytes:** ${formatNumber(manifest.totals.totalContentBytes)}  `,
    "",
    "## 1. Security Scan & Redaction",
    "",
    "Matched values are replaced in place in `parts/*.md`; no excerpt of a matched",
    "line is reproduced in this report or anywhere else in the snapshot.",
    "",
  ];

  if (secretFindings.length === 0) {
    lines.push("✅ **No secret-like patterns detected.** Clean security scan.", "");
  } else {
    const byRule = new Map<string, number>();
    for (const finding of secretFindings) {
      for (const rule of finding.rules) byRule.set(rule, (byRule.get(rule) ?? 0) + 1);
    }

    // One row per line, listing every rule that matched it. A line that matched two rules used
    // to produce two rows with the same file and line, which reads like two separate leaks.
    lines.push(
      `⚠️ **${formatNumber(secretFindings.length)} line(s) contain values that were redacted:**`,
      "",
      "| File | Line | Rules |",
      "| :--- | ---: | :--- |"
    );
    for (const finding of secretFindings) {
      lines.push(
        `| ${code(finding.path ?? "unknown")} | ${finding.line} | ${finding.rules.map(code).join(", ")} |`
      );
    }
    lines.push("", "### Matches by rule", "", "| Rule | Lines |", "| :--- | ---: |");
    for (const [rule, count] of Array.from(byRule.entries()).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])) {
      lines.push(`| ${code(rule)} | ${count} |`);
    }
    lines.push("");
  }

  // Parse diagnostics: the CLI already warns about every file whose AST summary was
  // incomplete, but the artifact an auditor reads carried none of it.
  const parseIssues = manifest.files.filter(
    (file) =>
      file.summary !== undefined &&
      typeof file.summary === "object" &&
      "parseErrors" in file.summary &&
      typeof (file.summary as { parseErrors?: unknown }).parseErrors === "number" &&
      (file.summary as { parseErrors: number }).parseErrors > 0
  );
  // A previewed or oversized file is deliberately not parsed for a structural summary, so
  // "every parsed file" is true but misleading next to the generation warning that the same file
  // "was not parsed". Naming the skipped count makes the two accounts agree.
  const unparsedPreviews = manifest.files.filter(
    (file) =>
      file.summary === undefined && (file.isPreviewTruncated === true || file.isLarge === true)
  ).length;
  lines.push("## 2. Parse & AST Diagnostics", "");
  if (parseIssues.length === 0) {
    if (unparsedPreviews > 0) {
      lines.push(
        `✅ No parse errors among the files that were parsed; ${formatNumber(unparsedPreviews)} ` +
          "file(s) were previewed or too large to parse and produced no structural summary.",
        ""
      );
    } else {
      lines.push("✅ Every parsed file produced a complete structural summary.", "");
    }
  } else {
    lines.push(
      `${parseIssues.length} file(s) produced an incomplete summary:`,
      "",
      "| File | Parse Errors |",
      "| :--- | ---: |"
    );
    for (const file of parseIssues) {
      const errors = (file.summary as { parseErrors: number }).parseErrors;
      lines.push(`| ${code(file.relativePath)} | ${errors} |`);
    }
    lines.push(
      "",
      "A parse error here means the summary is incomplete, not that the file is excluded —",
      "the content is still in the parts.",
      ""
    );
  }

  lines.push("## 3. Large Files Analysis", "");
  // Truncation has two independent causes: a file over `largeFileBytes` is reduced to a head/tail
  // preview before partitioning, and a file that then does not fit `maxPartBytes` is cut again by
  // the chunker. The old section counted only the first, so a snapshot whose previews were clipped
  // by the part budget reported "no files exceeded the large file budget" with a straight face.
  const largeFiles = manifest.files.filter((file) => file.isLarge);
  const truncatedFiles = manifest.files.filter((file) => file.isPreviewTruncated);
  const affected = manifest.files.filter((file) => file.isLarge || file.isPreviewTruncated);
  if (affected.length === 0) {
    lines.push("✅ No files exceeded the large file budget and none were truncated.", "");
  } else {
    lines.push(
      `Found ${formatNumber(largeFiles.length)} file(s) over the large-file budget (${(manifest.budgets.largeFileBytes / 1024).toFixed(0)} KB) and ${formatNumber(truncatedFiles.length)} file(s) truncated by a budget:`,
      "",
      "| File | Size (B) | Source Lines | Included Bytes | Included Lines | Status | Cut by |",
      "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |"
    );
    for (const file of affected) {
      const status = file.isPreviewTruncated ? "Truncated" : "Full";
      // `isLarge` is set by the size threshold before partitioning; anything false there was cut
      // by the part budget. A large file can additionally be clipped by the part budget.
      //
      // The cause is the setting that actually removed the bytes. `isLarge` alone said
      // `largeFileBytes` even when the part budget then cut the preview in half, which sent the
      // reader to raise the wrong number. The wording matches the marker in the block and the
      // `truncationReason` code in the manifest — one cause, one name — with the setting in
      // parentheses so it is still obvious what to change.
      const cutBy =
        file.truncationReason === "part-budget"
          ? "part budget (maxPartBytes)"
          : "large-file budget (largeFileBytes)";
      lines.push(
        `| ${code(file.relativePath)} | ${file.sizeBytes} | ${file.lines} | ${file.includedBytes} | ${file.includedLines} | ${status} | ${cutBy} |`
      );
    }
    lines.push(
      "",
      `Truncated: ${formatNumber(truncatedFiles.length)} of ${formatNumber(manifest.counts.totalFiles)} file(s); every truncated block carries a \`[N lines omitted]\` notice naming its budget.`,
      ""
    );
  }

  lines.push(
    "## 4. Architecture Layer Distribution",
    "",
    "| Layer | File Count | Percentage |",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- |"
  );
  const totalFiles = manifest.counts.totalFiles || 1;
  for (const [layer, count] of Object.entries(manifest.layers).sort(
    (a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || (a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : a[0] > b[0] ? 1 : 0)
  )) {
    const pct = ((count / totalFiles) * 100).toFixed(1);
    lines.push(`| ${code(layer)} | ${count} | ${pct}% |`);
  }
  lines.push("");

  lines.push(
    "## 5. File Kind Distribution",
    "",
    "| Kind | File Count | Percentage |",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- |"
  );
  for (const [kind, count] of Object.entries(manifest.kinds).sort(
    (a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || (a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : a[0] > b[0] ? 1 : 0)
  )) {
    const pct = ((count / totalFiles) * 100).toFixed(1);
    lines.push(`| ${code(kind)} | ${count} | ${pct}% |`);
  }
  lines.push("");

  lines.push("## 6. Skipped / Non-Text Files", "");
  if (manifest.skipped.length === 0) {
    lines.push("No files were skipped.", "");
  } else {
    lines.push(
      `Total skipped: ${manifest.skipped.length}`,
      "",
      "| File | Reason | Detail |",
      "| :--- | :--- | :--- |"
    );
    for (const skip of manifest.skipped) {
      lines.push(`| ${code(skip.path)} | ${code(skip.reason)} | ${tableCell(skip.detail ?? "-")} |`);
    }
    lines.push("");
  }

  return lines.join("\n");
}
</file>

<file path="src/generator/brief.ts">
import { createHash } from "node:crypto";

/**
 * The owner-written half of the brief.
 *
 * A generated brief can state the job, the map and the standard; it cannot say what the software
 * is for, what has already gone wrong, or which failures this team would recognise. Those are
 * judgement, so they are supplied as a file and spliced in at fixed slots.
 *
 * The parse is deliberately strict: a heading the merge does not know is kept under "Owner
 * notes" rather than dropped, and a heading that redefines a *measured* section is refused,
 * because letting prose overwrite the coverage or the fingerprint would make the document lie.
 */

/** Headings the merge understands, in the order they appear in the rendered brief. */
export const OWNER_SECTIONS = ["Stakes", "The system", "What came before", "The bar", "Calibration"] as const;

/** Headings that are measured, so the brief may not redefine them. */
export const RESERVED_SECTIONS = [
  "The job",
  "What you have",
  "Coverage",
  "What a useful answer contains",
  "This snapshot",
  "Attaching this",
  "Your task",
] as const;

export interface OwnerBrief {
  /** Path as the user wrote it, for provenance in the rendered brief. */
  path: string;
  /** sha256 of the file as read, so changing it is configuration drift. */
  digest: string;
  /** The body of each recognised heading, when the file has one. */
  stakes?: string;
  system?: string;
  history?: string;
  bar?: string;
  calibration?: string;
  /** Everything the merge did not recognise, kept rather than dropped. */
  notes: Array<{ heading: string; body: string }>;
  /** Text before the first heading, when there is any. */
  preamble?: string;
}

/**
 * Parses the owner brief and reports what cannot be honoured.
 *
 * Problems are returned rather than thrown so the caller can decide: `resolveConfig` turns them
 * into a configuration error, which is what a mistyped heading deserves.
 */
export function parseOwnerBrief(
  filePath: string,
  text: string
): { brief: OwnerBrief; problems: string[] } {
  const problems: string[] = [];
  // A byte-order mark is not a heading: a brief saved by a Windows editor would otherwise
  // lose its first section and produce a snapshot that silently omitted it.
  // Comments are guidance, not content: the scaffold `init` writes is nothing but comments,
  // and an untouched one must render no section rather than an empty one with a provenance line.
  const source = text.replace(/^\uFEFF/, "").replace(/<!--[\s\S]*?-->/g, "");
  const preamble: string[] = [];
  const sections: Array<{ heading: string; body: string[] }> = [];
  let current: { heading: string; body: string[] } | null = null;
  // A fenced code block can contain anything, including a line that looks like a heading. The
  // first version parsed one as a real section: a brief that *showed* the format with a sample
  // "## Coverage" was refused for redefining a measured section that it never defined.
  let fence: { char: string; length: number } | null = null;
  for (const line of source.split(/\r?\n/)) {
    const fenceLine = /^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})/.exec(line);
    if (fenceLine) {
      const marker = fenceLine[1] as string;
      const char = marker[0] as string;
      if (fence === null) fence = { char, length: marker.length };
      else if (char === fence.char && marker.length >= fence.length) fence = null;
      if (current) current.body.push(line);
      else preamble.push(line);
      continue;
    }
    if (fence !== null) {
      if (current) current.body.push(line);
      else preamble.push(line);
      continue;
    }
    const heading = /^##\s+(.+?)\s*$/.exec(line);
    if (heading) {
      current = { heading: heading[1] as string, body: [] };
      sections.push(current);
      continue;
    }
    if (current) current.body.push(line);
    else preamble.push(line);
  }

  const recognised: Record<string, string> = {};
  const notes: Array<{ heading: string; body: string }> = [];
  // Headings are matched case-insensitively, so "## coverage" cannot smuggle prose past the
  // reservation, and a heading defined twice is ambiguous rather than last-wins.
  const canonical = (heading: string): string =>
    heading
      .trim()
      // "## Stakes:" is the same intent as "## Stakes"; treating it as an unknown heading sent
      // the owner stakes to Owner notes while the generic prose rendered in their place.
      .replace(/[:.]+$/, "")
      .trim()
      .toLowerCase()
      .replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  const canonicalOwner = new Map(OWNER_SECTIONS.map((name) => [canonical(name), name]));
  const canonicalReserved = new Set(RESERVED_SECTIONS.map(canonical));
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  for (const section of sections) {
    const body = section.body.join("\n").trim();
    const key = canonical(section.heading);
    if (seen.has(key)) {
      problems.push(
        `the brief defines "${section.heading}" twice; a section cannot say two things.`
      );
      continue;
    }
    seen.add(key);
    if (canonicalReserved.has(key)) {
      problems.push(
        `the brief may not define "${section.heading}": that section is rendered from the manifest, ` +
          "and letting prose overwrite a measured section would make the document lie."
      );
      continue;
    }
    const ownerName = canonicalOwner.get(key);
    if (ownerName !== undefined) {
      // An empty section is a template someone has not filled in yet, not an assertion.
      if (body.length > 0) recognised[ownerName] = body;
      continue;
    }
    // Unknown headings are kept, not dropped: a brief is the owner's text, and silently
    // discarding a section would be the one thing this tool never does.
    notes.push({ heading: section.heading, body });
  }

  const preambleText = preamble.join("\n").trim();
  return {
    brief: {
      path: filePath,
      digest: createHash("sha256").update(text, "utf8").digest("hex"),
      ...(recognised["Stakes"] ? { stakes: recognised["Stakes"] } : {}),
      ...(recognised["The system"] ? { system: recognised["The system"] } : {}),
      ...(recognised["What came before"] ? { history: recognised["What came before"] } : {}),
      ...(recognised["The bar"] ? { bar: recognised["The bar"] } : {}),
      ...(recognised["Calibration"] ? { calibration: recognised["Calibration"] } : {}),
      notes,
      ...(preambleText.length > 0 ? { preamble: preambleText } : {}),
    },
    problems,
  };
}
</file>

<file path="src/mcp/protocol.ts">
/**
 * JSON-RPC 2.0 envelopes and the Model Context Protocol revisions this server speaks.
 *
 * Two protocol eras coexist and this file is the only place that knows which revision belongs
 * to which era. The legacy era (`2025-11-25` and earlier) opens every connection with an
 * `initialize` handshake. The modern era (`2026-07-28`) removed the handshake: every request
 * carries the protocol version and client capabilities in `_meta`, `server/discover` is
 * mandatory, every result carries `resultType`, and `ping` no longer exists.
 *
 * The constants are read by the negotiation in `era.ts`, by the dispatch in `server.ts` and by
 * the `describe` contract, so a revision cannot be added to one of them and forgotten in the
 * others.
 */

/** A JSON object as it appears on the wire: the shape every `params`/`result` takes. */
export type JsonObject = Record<string, unknown>;

export type JsonRpcId = string | number;

/** A request: has an `id` and expects exactly one response. */
export interface JsonRpcRequest {
  jsonrpc: "2.0";
  id: JsonRpcId;
  method: string;
  params?: JsonObject;
}

/** A notification: no `id`, and a response is a protocol violation. */
export interface JsonRpcNotification {
  jsonrpc: "2.0";
  method: string;
  params?: JsonObject;
}

export interface JsonRpcError {
  code: number;
  message: string;
  data?: unknown;
}

export interface JsonRpcSuccessResponse {
  jsonrpc: "2.0";
  id: JsonRpcId | null;
  result: JsonObject;
}

export interface JsonRpcErrorResponse {
  jsonrpc: "2.0";
  id: JsonRpcId | null;
  error: JsonRpcError;
}

export type JsonRpcResponse = JsonRpcSuccessResponse | JsonRpcErrorResponse;

/**
 * Revisions that open with the `initialize` handshake, newest first.
 *
 * The server echoes the requested revision when it is in this list and otherwise answers with
 * the newest entry: the legacy lifecycle says a server that cannot speak the requested version
 * responds with one it does speak, and the client decides whether to continue.
 */
export const LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS: readonly string[] = ["2025-11-25", "2025-06-18"];

/** Revisions that carry version and capabilities on every request, newest first. */
export const MODERN_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS: readonly string[] = ["2026-07-28"];

/** Everything advertised in `server/discover` and in an unsupported-version error. */
export const SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS: readonly string[] = [
  ...MODERN_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS,
  ...LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS,
];

/** The revision a legacy client gets when it asks for something this server cannot speak. */
export const DEFAULT_LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSION = LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS[0];

/**
 * JSON-RPC error codes, plus the one the modern era added.
 *
 * `-32022` is not a generic bad-params error: a client that receives it knows to read
 * `data.supported` and retry with a mutually supported revision. Reporting it as `-32602`
 * would leave a modern client with no way to recover.
 */
export const ERROR_CODES = {
  parseError: -32700,
  invalidRequest: -32600,
  methodNotFound: -32601,
  invalidParams: -32602,
  internalError: -32603,
  /**
   * A resource whose complete text is larger than one read may return.
   *
   * `-32000` is JSON-RPC's range for implementation-defined server errors, which is what this is:
   * there is no standard code for "the document is too large", and answering with -32602 would tell
   * a client its request was malformed when it was not. The message names the size, the limit and
   * the tool to use instead, because a resource read that cannot return the whole resource must not
   * invent a partial one.
   */
  resourceTooLarge: -32000,
  /**
   * A known resource that this snapshot does not contain.
   *
   * `-32002` is the MCP code for "resource not found". Answering `-32602` ("Unknown resource")
   * for `snapshot://prompt` in a snapshot generated with `--skip-artifacts prompt` told the
   * client its URI was wrong when the artifact had simply been left out.
   */
  resourceNotFound: -32002,
  /**
   * The server is at its concurrency limit.
   *
   * Implementation-defined range, like `resourceTooLarge`. A typed error rather than silent
   * queueing or a dropped request: the caller can retry, and knows why.
   */
  overloaded: -32001,
  unsupportedProtocolVersion: -32022,
} as const;

/** Method names, kept as constants so a typo is a compile error rather than a `-32601`. */
export const METHOD = {
  initialize: "initialize",
  initialized: "notifications/initialized",
  discover: "server/discover",
  ping: "ping",
  toolsList: "tools/list",
  toolsCall: "tools/call",
  resourcesList: "resources/list",
  resourcesRead: "resources/read",
  resourceTemplatesList: "resources/templates/list",
  promptsList: "prompts/list",
  promptsGet: "prompts/get",
  cancelled: "notifications/cancelled",
} as const;

/** `_meta` keys the modern era defines. */
export const META_KEYS = {
  protocolVersion: "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion",
  clientCapabilities: "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities",
  clientInfo: "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo",
  serverInfo: "io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo",
  progressToken: "progressToken",
} as const;

/** True for a plain JSON object, which is the only thing a `params`/`result` may be. */
export function isJsonObject(value: unknown): value is JsonObject {
  return typeof value === "object" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value);
}

/**
 * A JSON Schema subset, as far as this server needs it.
 *
 * Deliberately not `unknown`: the tool table is authored by hand, and the compiler catching a
 * misspelled keyword is worth more than accepting every dialect feature we never emit.
 */
export interface JsonSchema {
  type?: "object" | "string" | "number" | "integer" | "boolean" | "array";
  description?: string;
  properties?: Record<string, JsonSchema>;
  required?: string[];
  items?: JsonSchema;
  enum?: readonly string[];
  default?: string | number | boolean;
  additionalProperties?: boolean;
  minimum?: number;
  maximum?: number;
  minLength?: number;
  maxLength?: number;
}

/** An `inputSchema` root: MCP requires a top-level object schema. */
export interface InputSchema extends JsonSchema {
  type: "object";
  properties: Record<string, JsonSchema>;
}

/**
 * `_meta` every result carries in the modern era.
 *
 * A server identifies itself on each result rather than once at handshake, because there is no
 * handshake any more. The legacy era has no equivalent and must not receive this field.
 */
export function serverInfoMeta(info: {
  name: string;
  title?: string;
  version: string;
}): JsonObject {
  const value: JsonObject = { name: info.name, version: info.version };
  if (info.title) value.title = info.title;
  return { [META_KEYS.serverInfo]: value };
}

/** Builds a successful response. `modern` decides whether `resultType` and `_meta` appear. */
export function successResponse(
  id: JsonRpcId | null,
  result: JsonObject,
  modern: boolean,
  info: { name: string; title?: string; version: string }
): JsonRpcSuccessResponse {
  if (!modern) return { jsonrpc: "2.0", id, result };
  const withMeta: JsonObject = {
    ...result,
    resultType: "complete",
    _meta: { ...(isJsonObject(result._meta) ? result._meta : {}), ...serverInfoMeta(info) },
  };
  return { jsonrpc: "2.0", id, result: withMeta };
}

/** Builds an error response. Errors are identical in both eras. */
export function errorResponse(
  id: JsonRpcId | null,
  code: number,
  message: string,
  data?: unknown
): JsonRpcErrorResponse {
  const error: JsonRpcError = { code, message };
  if (data !== undefined) error.data = data;
  return { jsonrpc: "2.0", id, error };
}
</file>

<file path="src/mcp/retrieval.ts">
import type { FileEntry, Manifest } from "../types.js";

/**
 * The retrieval planner: which files answer a question, within a budget.
 *
 * This is the reason the product exists. A snapshot is a dump; a plan is an answer to "given this
 * question and 30k tokens, what should the reader actually open?" The plan is deterministic, it
 * explains every file it includes, and it never invents a path the snapshot does not carry.
 */

export interface PlannedFile {
  path: string;
  part: string;
  layer: string;
  kind: string;
  tokens: number;
  /** full body, or head only when the file is large relative to the budget. */
  mode: "full" | "head";
  /** Why it is here: which term matched, or which file pulled it in. */
  why: string;
}

export interface PlanForResult {
  question: string;
  budgetTokens: number;
  usedTokens: number;
  files: PlannedFile[];
  skipped: Array<{ path: string; reason: string }>;
  notes: string[];
}

const STOPWORDS = new Set([
  "the", "and", "for", "with", "that", "this", "from", "what", "when", "where", "which", "how",
  "why", "does", "did", "are", "was", "were", "has", "have", "had", "can", "could", "should",
  "would", "will", "into", "about", "over", "under", "then", "than", "there", "here", "its",
  "it", "is", "a", "an", "of", "to", "in", "on", "at", "by", "or", "be", "not", "do", "if",
]);

function termsOf(question: string): string[] {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  for (const raw of question.toLowerCase().split(/[^a-z0-9_]+/)) {
    if (raw.length < 3 || STOPWORDS.has(raw)) continue;
    seen.add(raw);
    if (seen.size >= 40) break;
  }
  return [...seen];
}

/** Names a file exports or defines, lowercased. */
function symbolNames(file: FileEntry): string[] {
  const summary = file.summary;
  if (summary === undefined) return [];
  const names = new Set<string>();
  const record = summary as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;
  for (const key of ["exports", "functions", "classes", "interfaces", "types", "enums", "variables"]) {
    const value = record[key];
    if (!Array.isArray(value)) continue;
    for (const item of value) {
      if (typeof item === "string" && item.length > 1) names.add(item.toLowerCase());
    }
  }
  return [...names];
}

function tokensOf(file: FileEntry): number {
  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(file.includedBytes / 4));
}

function isTest(file: FileEntry): boolean {
  return (
    file.layer === "test" ||
    /(^|\/)(tests?|__tests__|spec)\//.test(file.relativePath) ||
    /\.(test|spec)\.[a-z0-9]+$/i.test(file.relativePath)
  );
}

/** The files a specifier plausibly names, by suffix rather than by resolver. */
function resolveImport(from: string, specifier: string, byPath: Map<string, FileEntry>): FileEntry | null {
  if (!specifier.startsWith(".")) return null;
  const base = from.includes("/") ? from.slice(0, from.lastIndexOf("/")) : "";
  const joined = specifier.startsWith("/") ? specifier.slice(1) : base + "/" + specifier;
  const parts: string[] = [];
  for (const segment of joined.split("/")) {
    if (segment === "" || segment === ".") continue;
    if (segment === "..") parts.pop();
    else parts.push(segment);
  }
  const path = parts.join("/");
  // A TypeScript repository writes "./user.js" and means "./user.ts"; extension-less specifiers
  // are common too. Both are tried before giving up, or the caller side of the graph is empty.
  const withoutExtension = path.replace(/\.[cm]?[jt]sx?$/, "");
  const candidates = [
    path,
    withoutExtension + ".ts",
    withoutExtension + ".tsx",
    withoutExtension + ".js",
    withoutExtension + ".jsx",
    withoutExtension + ".py",
    withoutExtension + ".go",
    withoutExtension + ".rs",
    withoutExtension + "/index.ts",
    path + ".ts",
    path + ".tsx",
    path + ".js",
    path + ".jsx",
    path + ".mjs",
    path + ".cjs",
    path + ".py",
    path + ".go",
    path + ".rs",
    path + ".java",
    path + "/index.ts",
    path + "/index.js",
  ];
  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    const found = byPath.get(candidate);
    if (found !== undefined) return found;
  }
  return null;
}

interface Scored {
  file: FileEntry;
  score: number;
  why: string;
}

export function planForQuestion(
  manifest: Manifest,
  question: string,
  options: { maxTokens?: number; includeTests?: boolean } = {}
): PlanForResult {
  const budget = Math.max(500, options.maxTokens ?? 30000);
  const terms = termsOf(question);
  const byPath = new Map(manifest.files.map((file) => [file.relativePath, file]));
  const importers = new Map<string, string[]>();
  const importsOf = new Map<string, string[]>();
  for (const entry of manifest.indexes.imports.byFile) {
    importsOf.set(entry.path, entry.imports);
    for (const specifier of entry.imports) {
      const target = resolveImport(entry.path, specifier, byPath);
      if (target === null) continue;
      const list = importers.get(target.relativePath);
      if (list === undefined) importers.set(target.relativePath, [entry.path]);
      else list.push(entry.path);
    }
  }
  const centrality = new Map(manifest.indexes.imports.centralFiles.map((entry) => [entry.path, entry.importedByCount]));
  const wantsTests = options.includeTests === true || terms.some((term) => term === "test" || term === "tests" || term === "spec");

  const scored: Scored[] = [];
  for (const file of manifest.files) {
    if (isTest(file) && !wantsTests) continue;
    const path = file.relativePath.toLowerCase();
    const words = path.split(/[^a-z0-9_]+/).filter((word) => word.length >= 3);
    let score = 0;
    const reasons: string[] = [];
    for (const term of terms) {
      if (path.includes(term)) {
        score += 8;
        reasons.push("path:" + term);
        continue;
      }
      // The question says "authenticated"; the file is "auth.ts". A term that merely contains a
      // path word (or the reverse) is the same subject, and exact substring matching missed it.
      const partial = words.find(
        (word) => word.length >= 3 && (term.startsWith(word) || word.startsWith(term))
      );
      if (partial !== undefined) {
        score += 6;
        reasons.push("path:" + partial);
      }
    }
    for (const name of symbolNames(file)) {
      for (const term of terms) {
        // `charge` and `chargeCard` are the same subject; so are `authenticated` and `auth`.
        // Exact substring matching alone missed both, which made a real question plan nothing.
        const shared = Math.min(name.length, term.length);
        if (
          name.includes(term) ||
          term.includes(name) ||
          (shared >= 4 && name.slice(0, shared) === term.slice(0, shared))
        ) {
          score += 5;
          reasons.push("symbol:" + term);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    if (terms.includes(file.layer.toLowerCase())) {
      score += 2;
      reasons.push("layer:" + file.layer);
    }
    if (terms.includes(file.kind.toLowerCase())) {
      score += 2;
      reasons.push("kind:" + file.kind);
    }
    const hub = centrality.get(file.relativePath) ?? 0;
    score += Math.min(5, hub / 10);
    if (score <= 0) continue;
    scored.push({ file, score, why: reasons.slice(0, 3).join(", ") || "centrality " + hub });
  }

  // A question that matches nothing still deserves an answer: the entry points and hubs are the
  // honest fallback, and the notes say that is what happened.
  const notes: string[] = [];
  if (scored.length === 0) {
    notes.push("No file matched the question's terms; the plan falls back to the most-imported files.");
    const hubs = manifest.indexes.imports.centralFiles.slice(0, 12);
    for (const hub of hubs) {
      const file = byPath.get(hub.path);
      if (file !== undefined) scored.push({ file, score: 1, why: "hub, " + hub.importedByCount + " importers" });
    }
  }

  scored.sort((left, right) => right.score - left.score || left.file.relativePath.localeCompare(right.file.relativePath));

  const chosen = new Map<string, PlannedFile>();
  const skipped: Array<{ path: string; reason: string }> = [];
  let used = 0;

  const take = (file: FileEntry, why: string, mode: "full" | "head"): boolean => {
    const existing = chosen.get(file.relativePath);
    if (existing !== undefined) return false;
    const tokens = tokensOf(file);
    const cost = mode === "head" ? Math.ceil(tokens / 2) : tokens;
    if (used + cost > budget) return false;
    used += cost;
    chosen.set(file.relativePath, {
      path: file.relativePath,
      part: file.part,
      layer: file.layer,
      kind: file.kind,
      tokens: cost,
      mode,
      why,
    });
    return true;
  };

  for (const item of scored) {
    const tokens = tokensOf(item.file);
    if (used + tokens <= budget) {
      take(item.file, item.why, "full");
      continue;
    }
    // A file that is large relative to what is left is worth its head: the imports and signatures
    // at the top are usually enough to answer "does this matter?", and a skip answer is not.
    if (tokens <= budget * 2 && used + Math.ceil(tokens / 2) <= budget) {
      take(item.file, item.why, "head");
      continue;
    }
    skipped.push({ path: item.file.relativePath, reason: "does not fit the remaining budget" });
  }

  // Pull in the imports of the strongest matches: a file's own dependencies are usually the next
  // question, and they are cheap compared with a second round trip.
  const seeded = [...chosen.values()].slice(0, 6);
  for (const seed of seeded) {
    for (const specifier of importsOf.get(seed.path) ?? []) {
      const target = resolveImport(seed.path, specifier, byPath);
      if (target === null || chosen.has(target.relativePath)) continue;
      if (isTest(target) && !wantsTests) continue;
      if (!take(target, "imported by " + seed.path, "full")) {
        if (!take(target, "imported by " + seed.path, "head")) {
          skipped.push({ path: target.relativePath, reason: "does not fit the remaining budget" });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Then the callers of the strongest matches: "who uses this" is the other half of "what does
  // this use", and a plan that answers only the first half sends the reader to edit a function
  // without knowing what depends on it. Two per seed, capped, so the section cannot crowd out
  // the answer.
  let callerBudget = 4;
  for (const seed of [...chosen.values()].slice(0, 3)) {
    for (const caller of importers.get(seed.path) ?? []) {
      if (callerBudget <= 0) break;
      if (chosen.has(caller)) continue;
      const file = byPath.get(caller);
      if (file === undefined) continue;
      if (isTest(file) && !wantsTests) continue;
      if (take(file, "imports " + seed.path, "full")) {
        callerBudget -= 1;
      } else if (take(file, "imports " + seed.path, "head")) {
        callerBudget -= 1;
      }
    }
  }

  if (chosen.size === 0) notes.push("The budget is too small for any file in this snapshot.");
  notes.push(
    formatNumber(chosen.size) + " file(s) chosen of " +
      formatNumber(manifest.files.length) + " in the snapshot; " +
      formatNumber(skipped.length) + " skipped for budget."
  );
  if (!wantsTests) notes.push("Tests were excluded; ask for them explicitly to include them.");

  return {
    question,
    budgetTokens: budget,
    usedTokens: used,
    files: [...chosen.values()],
    skipped: skipped.slice(0, 20),
    notes,
  };
}

function formatNumber(value: number): string {
  return String(value).replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+(?!\d))/g, ",");
}
