# codebase-snapshot

> Turn any repository into an LLM-ready snapshot: code packed into size-budgeted markdown
> parts, indexed by a JSON manifest that records every included file and every skipped one with
> the reason, with secret redaction, and a `validate` command that fails when the code has moved.
> The MCP server serves that snapshot as 21 tools. Works on TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go,
> Rust, Java/Kotlin, C#/.NET, PHP, Ruby, C/C++, Vue, Svelte and Astro.

`codebase-snapshot` is a Node.js CLI and MCP server (Node >= 18, one runtime dependency:
`typescript`). It is for handing a whole repository to a language model as context: it packs
source files into markdown parts that never exceed a byte budget, records every included file and
every excluded file (with the reason) in `manifest.json`, lists every file whose body is not the
whole file in `incomplete[]` with its missing line ranges, masks secret values in place, and
reports large files and git state. `validate` re-hashes the tree and exits non-zero when the snapshot no
longer matches it, which makes "is this context still current?" a checkable question.

## Start here

- [Agent guide](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/agent.md): the machine-readable contract and the installable agent skill; the recommended entry point for an AI agent.
- [Installation](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/install.md): every npm-compatible client, version pinning, troubleshooting a shadowed `npx`.
- [CLI reference](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/cli.md): every command, every option with its default, exit codes, examples, and what the guided first run asks.
- [Configuration](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/configuration.md): every setting and default, the 25 project presets, byte budgets, artifacts, hooks, secret handling.
- [Snapshot output](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/output.md): directory layout and the complete `manifest.json` schema.
- [Troubleshooting](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/troubleshooting.md): every message the tool prints and what to do about it.
- [Changelog](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md): release history, breaking changes.

## Machine-readable entry points

- `snapshot` (after a global install) or `npx codebase-snapshot` — the guided first run. It reports the preset it detected and the file that decided it, asks one question, then writes the config, adds the npm scripts + devDependency + .gitignore entries, installs, and generates. A project that already has a config, or that generated before, just generates. **Unattended it exits 2 rather than guessing** and names the alternatives.
- `npx codebase-snapshot --autopilot` — the same first run with no questions, for CI, containers and agents. Keeps an existing config; `--no-install` skips the install.
- `npx codebase-snapshot setup` — the same one-command setup spelled as a command word: write the config (kept if one exists), add the npm scripts + devDependency + .gitignore entries, install dependencies with the project's package manager, then generate. `--no-install` skips the install; `--dry-run` writes nothing.
- `npx codebase-snapshot describe --format json` — the full contract as JSON on stdout: invocation, ordered workflow, commands, options with defaults, exit codes, presets, artifacts, config settings, output layout, safety rules, troubleshooting.
- `npx codebase-snapshot skill` — the bundled agent skill, printed on stdout.
- `npx codebase-snapshot skill --install` — writes the skill to the two registries agent tools read by name: `.claude/skills/codebase-snapshot/SKILL.md` and `.cursor/rules/codebase-snapshot.mdc`, and adds them to `.gitignore`. It never writes `AGENTS.md`; that file is the repository's own agent contract, so print the skill and paste it if you want it there.
- [package.json](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/package.json) — npm metadata, keywords and entry points.
- npm registry: https://registry.npmjs.org/codebase-snapshot

## Typical use

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot init --yes   # detect the project, write a config
npx codebase-snapshot generate     # write ./snapshot/
npx codebase-snapshot validate     # exit 0 = snapshot matches the files on disk
```

Exit codes: `0` success, `1` drift, refused generation, or nothing to snapshot, `2` usage error —
including a first run that needs an answer and has no terminal. Human progress goes
to stderr; `--format json` payloads go to stdout and are safe to parse. A failing JSON-mode run
still writes one parseable document — `{"error":{"message":…,"exitCode":…}}` — so an agent never
has to parse an empty string or scrape stderr for prose.

## Notes for agents

- Pin an exact version — `npx codebase-snapshot --version` prints the current one — when the same version must run on every machine. The bare package name
  also works — both bin entries (`codebase-snapshot`, `snapshot`) point at the same file, which is what lets npm resolve it.
- **Do not run the bare command expecting it to just generate.** In a project with no configuration it exits 2 without a terminal. Say what you want: `generate` for the documented defaults, `--autopilot` for the full setup, or `init --yes` then `generate` when you want to see each step.
- **If discovery refuses** ("This directory is a git repository, but git could not list its
  files"), the fix is `--discovery walk`: it scans the tree directly and reads `.gitignore` files
  itself. `--discovery git` is the opposite — require git and refuse without it. The default,
  `auto`, uses git when it works and refuses rather than silently changing what the snapshot
  contains.
- The output directory is owned by the tool and is replaced atomically. It refuses to replace a
  directory it did not write, so never point `--out-dir` at source code.
- Credential files (`.env`, `*.pem`, `.npmrc`, `.ssh/`, `.tfstate`) are excluded unless
  `--include-sensitive-files` is given, and they are recorded in `manifest.skipped[]`.
- Secret-shaped strings inside ordinary files are redacted in the emitted parts; no excerpt of a
  matched line reaches any artifact. The count of masked **lines** is `manifest.totals.redactedLines`
  and the per-line table (file, line, every rule that matched) is `reports/audit.md` section 1.
  Redactions are reported separately from `manifest.warnings` — a masked value is the tool working,
  not a defect, so read `Redacted:` and `Warnings:` as different signals.
- Redaction is fail-closed: a `.env.example` placeholder (`JWT_SECRET=replace-with-…`) and a test
  literal (`process.env.X_SECRET = "test-value"`) are masked like live keys. Expect templates to be
  less readable inside the snapshot; do not "fix" that by re-reading the raw file into context.
- `manifest.files[]` carries `encoding` when a file was not plain UTF-8: `utf-16le`/`utf-16be` were
  transcoded to text, and `utf-8-lossy` means the body contains `U+FFFD` where undecodable bytes
  were. Its absence means clean UTF-8.
- `manifest.skipped[]` names a reason for every excluded path — `binary`, `gitignore`,
  `default-ignore`, `sensitive-file`, `sensitive-path`, `symlink`, `outside-root`, `read-error`,
  `not-file`, `nested-repository` (a submodule or nested checkout) or `excluded`. `nested-repository`
  and `not-file` only appear for candidates git listed as a directory.

## Optional

- [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/AGENTS.md): conventions for contributing to this repository.
- [Wiki](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/wiki): a pointer to the documentation site.
- [Releases](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/releases): version history with notes.
- [Feeding a codebase to ChatGPT, Claude or Cursor](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/how-to-feed-a-repo-to-claude.md): the end-to-end recipe, including what the model should be told about the snapshot it is reading.
- [Stale LLM context is a bug](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/stale-context-is-your-bug.md): why a snapshot goes out of date silently, and the fingerprint/validate/gate controls that make drift visible.
- [How many tokens is a codebase?](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/codebase-token-budget.md): the arithmetic of bytes, tokens and context windows, with a measured example and a budget table.
- [Budgeting LLM context](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/how-to-context-budget.md): how to choose `maxPartBytes`, `largeFileBytes` and per-layer ceilings from the consumer rather than from taste.
- [vs repomix](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/vs-repomix.md), [vs gitingest](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/vs-gitingest.md), [vs code2prompt](https://github.com/ankurhalder/codebase-snapshot/blob/main/docs/vs-code2prompt.md): honest comparisons that say where each alternative is the better choice.
</file>

<file path="src/mcp/types.ts">
/**
 * The capability records the MCP server serves.
 *
 * These are the shapes the protocol layer deals in, kept apart from the handlers that fill them so
 * the server can be tested with a fixed set of stubs and the tool table can be built without a
 * running transport.
 */
import type { InputSchema, JsonObject } from "./protocol.js";

/** Everything a handler may touch. Deliberately small: no transport, no session, no protocol. */
export interface ToolContext {
  /** Repository the server was launched for; every relative path resolves against it. */
  cwd: string;
  /** Absolute directories a `root` argument is allowed to resolve into. */
  roots: string[];
  /** Writes one line to stderr. Never stdout, which carries protocol messages only. */
  log: (line: string) => void;
  /**
   * Emits a progress notification for the current call.
   *
   * Present only when the client supplied a `progressToken` in `_meta` and the transport can carry
   * a notification mid-call (stdio). A tool that never calls it is still correct; a tool that does
   * gives a long run a heartbeat instead of a silent pause.
   */
  notify?: (event: { message: string; progress?: number; total?: number }) => void;
  /**
   * Registers a callback the server runs when the client cancels this call.
   *
   * A tool that can stop early (a generate terminates its worker) calls this once with the function
   * that stops it. A tool that cannot stops nothing, and the server simply runs it to completion.
   */
  onCancel?: (cancel: () => void) => void;
}

/**
 * Client-facing hints.
 *
 * `readOnlyHint` is the one that changes behaviour rather than appearance: clients use it to
 * auto-approve a call. Seven of the 21 tools never touch the filesystem, so they are marked
 * read-only and only `snapshot_generate` prompts.
 */
export interface ToolAnnotations {
  readOnlyHint: boolean;
  destructiveHint: boolean;
  idempotentHint: boolean;
  openWorldHint: boolean;
}

/** What a handler returns. The protocol layer turns this into a `CallToolResult`. */
export interface ToolOutcome {
  /** The text block a model reads. Keep it short: bulk content belongs in a windowed read. */
  text: string;
  /** Typed mirror of `text`, declared by the tool's `outputSchema`. */
  structured?: JsonObject;
  /** True for a tool-level failure. Reported inside the result, not as a JSON-RPC error. */
  isError?: boolean;
}

/**
 * How expensive a tool call is, in the units an agent can act on.
 *
 * `cheap` reads the manifest; `medium` reads one or a few parts; `expensive` reads the repository,
 * partitions it, or writes. The class is a hint for choosing between two tools that answer the
 * same question, and it is stated as a class because a token count depends on the repository.
 */
export type ToolCost = "cheap" | "medium" | "expensive";

export interface McpTool {
  name: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  inputSchema: InputSchema;
  outputSchema?: InputSchema;
  annotations: ToolAnnotations;
  /** Relative cost of a call; see `ToolCost`. */
  cost: ToolCost;
  handler(args: JsonObject, context: ToolContext): Promise<ToolOutcome>;
}

/** One addressable resource, as `resources/list` reports it. */
export interface McpResourceDescriptor {
  uri: string;
  name: string;
  title?: string;
  description: string;
  mimeType: string;
}

export interface McpResourceContents {
  uri: string;
  mimeType: string;
  text: string;
}

/** A parameterised resource family, as `resources/templates/list` reports it. */
export interface McpResourceTemplate {
  uriTemplate: string;
  name: string;
  title?: string;
  description: string;
  mimeType: string;
}

/**
 * Resources are computed per call rather than declared once.
 *
 * The list depends on what is on disk — a snapshot that has not been generated has manifest
 * resources that do not exist yet — so a static table would advertise resources whose read then
 * fails, which is worse than an honest short list.
 */
export interface ResourceProvider {
  list(context: ToolContext): Promise<McpResourceDescriptor[]>;
  templates(): McpResourceTemplate[];
  /** Resolves one URI. Returns null when this server does not own it. */
  read(uri: string, context: ToolContext): Promise<McpResourceContents | null>;
}

export interface PromptArgument {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  required: boolean;
}

export interface PromptMessage {
  role: "user" | "assistant";
  content: { type: "text"; text: string };
}

export interface McpPrompt {
  name: string;
  title?: string;
  description: string;
  arguments: PromptArgument[];
  build(args: JsonObject): { description: string; messages: PromptMessage[] };
}

/** Identity reported at handshake and in every modern result's `_meta`. */
export interface McpServerInfo {
  name: string;
  title: string;
  version: string;
  websiteUrl: string;
}
</file>

<file path="AGENTS.md">
# AGENTS.md

Guidance for AI agents working **in this repository**, and for agents that consume the
published package.

## Consuming the package

The package ships its own agent skill and a machine-readable contract. Do not guess commands:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot describe --format json   # the full contract
npx codebase-snapshot skill                   # the skill, on stdout
npx codebase-snapshot skill --install         # into .claude/skills + .cursor/rules
```

Read [docs/agent.md](docs/agent.md) first. In short: `setup` — config, scripts, dependency, install
and generate in one command — or step by step: `init --yes` → `generate` → `validate`.
`generate --autopilot` is the same one-command path from the default command.
Pin an exact version — `npx codebase-snapshot --version` prints the current one — when the same version must run everywhere.

**The bare command is not the safe default for a machine.** In a project with no configuration,
`npx codebase-snapshot` with no command word asks one question, and with no terminal it exits `2`
instead of generating with defaults nobody chose. That is deliberate (see rule 1 below): name what
you want — `generate`, `--autopilot`, or `init --yes` — rather than retrying the same command.

`skill --install` deliberately does **not** write `AGENTS.md`. That file is a repository's own
agent contract, and this tool has no business editing one — it installs into the two registries
keyed by skill name, and gitignores what it creates. An agent file that wants the skill in it
should paste the output of `skill`, so the commit belongs to that repository.

The installable skill's canonical text lives in `src/agent/skill.ts` and is rendered by
`codebase-snapshot skill` — do not hand-edit a generated copy.

If the agent runs inside an MCP client, `npx codebase-snapshot mcp` serves the repository as 21
tools over stdio instead of shelling out. Reads come from the snapshot rather than the working
tree, so excluded and redacted content stays out of context. See [docs/mcp.md](docs/mcp.md).

## Working in this repository

**Stack.** TypeScript (strict, `noUnusedLocals`, `noUnusedParameters`, ES2022, NodeNext),
bundled with tsup to ESM + CJS + type definitions. Tests use `node --test`. The only runtime
dependency is `typescript`; keep it that way — the zero-dependency property is a feature.

**Layout.**

| Path | Contains |
| :--- | :--- |
| `src/index.ts` | `generateSnapshot`, the public surface, output-directory guards |
| `src/cli.ts` | argument parsing, every command, exit codes |
| `src/config.ts` | config discovery, validation, `resolveConfig` |
| `src/scanner/` | file collection, git info, ignores |
| `src/ast/` | TypeScript parsing and the language profiles |
| `src/presets/` | the 25 presets and detection |
| `src/partitioner/` | budgeting and part rendering |
| `src/wizard/` | interactive setup and the config template |
| `src/agent/` | the machine contract and the bundled skill |
| `src/mcp/` | the MCP server: protocol, transport, tools, resources, prompts |
| `src/validator/` | drift detection |
| `tests/` | unit and integration suites |

**Rules that are load-bearing — do not break them:**

1. **No silent fallbacks.** An unknown preset, an unparseable config, an invalid budget and an
   unrecognised flag are all hard errors. If a setting cannot be applied, say so.
2. **Never destroy user data.** The output directory is only replaceable when this tool wrote
   it, and the proof is evidence rather than a filename: `.codebase-snapshot` is a JSON record
   (generator, schema version, run id, state, manifest digest, inventory of every file written)
   and the directory may contain nothing outside that inventory. A hand-written marker whose
   recorded digests do not match the directory is not evidence. When Windows or OneDrive will
   not rename a directory whose handle someone else holds, the swap removes that
   guard-cleared directory and replaces it in place — the same rule by a different mechanism,
   and only after the marker has been checked.
3. **Never leak secrets.** Sensitive files stay out unless explicitly requested; redaction masks
   only the matched span and no excerpt of a matched line reaches an artifact.
4. **Progress to stderr, payload to stdout.** `--format json` must keep stdout parseable.
5. **The contract must not lie.** `describe` is generated from code, and a test compares its
   defaults against a real `generate --dry-run` manifest. Update both together. A file's
   `inclusionMode` says *what was written*, `truncationReason` says *which rule removed the
   bytes*, and `omittedRanges` says *where*: a preview with no reason is a lie of omission,
   and `verifyInMemory` fails the run for it.
6. **Nothing disappears without a recorded reason.** Every scanned path is in `files[]` or in
   `skipped[]`, and `verifyInMemory` asserts it. If you add a path that can be dropped, add its
   skip record in the same change.

**Before you commit.**

```bash
npm run check     # lint + typecheck + tests
npm run build
```

`npm test` runs the suite through `scripts/test-run.mjs`: it points `TEMP`/`TMP`/`TMPDIR` at
one scratch root and deletes that root when the run ends, so a test that forgets its cleanup
leaves nothing behind. Create scratch trees with `makeTempDir(t, prefix)` from
`tests/helpers/temp.js` so the test's own `t.after` removes them.

`scripts/lint.mjs` enforces LF endings, no `any` in `src`, console usage only in
`src/cli.ts`/`src/bin.ts`/`scripts/`, and comment/formatting rules. `npm run verify` is
lint + typecheck + tests + a tarball check, and is exactly what the publish workflow runs
before it uploads — there is no separate CI matrix to wait for.

**Windows note.** The repository ships `.gitattributes` with `eol=lf`; a checkout with
`core.autocrlf=true` will otherwise fail the lint rule on untouched files.

## Versions

**The line runs `0.12.x` to `0.17.0`, with literal sub-bumps.** Every change ships as its own
version — `0.12.15`, `0.12.16`, … up to `0.12.99`, then `0.13.0` and the same ladder again. The
number is not the promise; the changelog entry is. Never `1.0.0`. `sync-docs` keeps every marked
surface at the same value, and `pack-check` fails when the lockfile disagrees.

Tags are created for every version, and `main` carries every commit. **npm publication happens
only at `x.y.0`** (`0.13.0`, `0.14.0`, …): the registry stays clean while the repository keeps a
complete, buildable history. `publish-train.yml` exists for republishing a specific tag; it is
not used for routine patches.

## Conventions

- Commits: `type: summary` (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `ci`, `chore`), with a body explaining the
  *why* and what was verified. Breaking changes get a `!`.
- Documentation changes belong with the behaviour change, in the same commit.
- Prefer a narrow fix with a test over a broad refactor without one.
</file>

<file path="docs/vs-repomix.md">
# codebase-snapshot vs repomix

Both tools answer the same question — *how do I hand a whole repository to a model that cannot
clone it?* They disagree about almost everything else. This page states where each one is the
better choice, including where repomix clearly is.

> Written by the author of `codebase-snapshot`. Numbers are the maintained project's own claims at
> the time of writing, and the comparison is deliberately unkind to this project where it deserves
> it.

## The short version

**repomix** packs a repository into an AI-friendly file, or several with `--split-output`. It is the
established tool in this space by a wide margin — tens of thousands of stars, an MCP server, remote
repository URLs, a token budget that fails a build, and an ecosystem of integrations.

**codebase-snapshot** produces *size-budgeted parts* rather than one file, and treats the snapshot
as something that can go stale and be proven current. Its distinguishing features are a hard byte
ceiling per part, deterministic output, and `validate`.

If you want the most popular, most integrated tool: **use repomix.**

## Side by side

| | codebase-snapshot | repomix |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Output shape | Multiple parts, each under a hard byte budget, plus a JSON manifest | One packed file (XML, Markdown or JSON), or several with `--split-output <size>` |
| Size control | **Yes** — `maxPartBytes` is a per-part byte ceiling; a file that cannot fit one part becomes a head/tail preview listed in `incomplete[]` | `--split-output <size>` splits by output size, and `--token-budget <n>` exits non-zero above a token count |
| Reproducible output | Parts are byte-identical across runs; a fingerprint covers them | Content is stable; no published determinism guarantee |
| Staleness check | `validate` re-hashes the repository and exits non-zero on drift | Not a feature |
| Remote repositories | No — operates on a local checkout | **Yes** — packs a GitHub URL directly |
| MCP server | **Yes** — 21 tools over stdio, and it serves the validated snapshot rather than the working tree | **Yes** — packs a repository, including by URL |
| Secret handling | 32 default rules, value-span masking, per-line audit table | Secretlint integration |
| Language awareness | 25 presets, AST or structural summaries labelled by confidence | Tree-sitter parsing with a compression mode |
| Adoption | New | **Mature** — large user base, many integrations |
| Runtime | Node.js ≥ 18, one dependency | Node.js, npm package |

## Where repomix is the better choice

- **You want one file.** Pasting a single artifact into a chat is simpler than assembling parts, and
  repomix is built around that workflow.
- **You need a remote URL.** `repomix --remote https://github.com/owner/repo` works without a clone.
  This project has no equivalent.
- **You want MCP against a remote URL.** repomix's server can pack a repository by URL. This one
  serves a local checkout, and refuses to read outside it. Both ship a server; ours is narrower on
  purpose, and it can tell the agent whether the snapshot it is reading is still current.
- **You want the ecosystem.** More stars, more tutorials, more people who have already hit your
  problem.
- **Your limit is a token count, not a byte count.** `--token-budget <n>` fails the run when the
  packed output exceeds a tokenizer's count, which is the number a context window actually measures.
  This project budgets bytes per part: exact, cheap to enforce, and one conversion away from the
  token count you care about.
- **You are packing something enormous and prefer compression to budgets.** repomix's tree-sitter
  compression mode is a different bet — shrink the content — and on very large repositories it may
  suit you better than partitioning.

## Where this is the better choice

- **You need a guarantee, not a best effort.** `maxPartBytes` promises no part exceeds the ceiling;
  a file that cannot fit one part is a head/tail preview with an explicit marker, and the manifest
  lists it in `incomplete[]` with the line ranges it lost. If a downstream system rejects payloads
  above a size, a warning is not enough.
- **You need to know the snapshot still matches the code.** `validate` compares every file hash
  *and* a digest of the settings the snapshot was generated with, so a changed budget or secret
  pattern counts as drift. That makes "is my context current?" a CI-gateable question.
- **You need byte-identical output.** Two runs over unchanged input produce identical `parts/`, so a
  snapshot can live in review, be diffed, or be cached by hash.
- **You need a machine index.** `manifest.json` records every included file *and every excluded file
  with a reason*, which is what makes `explain <path>` able to answer "why is this not in here?".
- **You want the tool to be drivable by an agent without documentation.** `describe --format json`
  emits the whole contract, and `skill --install` writes a skill into the registries agent tools
  read.

## Both at once

There is no conflict. A common arrangement is to use this tool for the *budgeted, validated* case
(CI, RAG ingestion, anything automated) and repomix when a human wants one file to paste into a
chat. They read the same repository and write to different directories.

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot setup          # config, scripts, dependency, install, generate
npx codebase-snapshot validate       # exit 0 means the snapshot matches the disk
```

## Honest summary

repomix is the safer recommendation for most people today, because maturity and integrations matter
more than any single feature. This tool exists for the narrower case where a size ceiling, a drift
check and reproducibility are requirements rather than nice-to-haves — and for the case where an
agent has to operate it without a human reading the docs first.
