# Configuration reference

Every setting, every default, and what each one changes. For commands and options see the
[CLI reference](cli.md).

Nothing here is required: with no config at all the tool runs on the defaults below.

## Contents

- [Config files](#config-files)
- [Scoping: which files get in](#scoping-which-files-get-in)
- [Project presets](#project-presets)
- [Classification: layers and kinds](#classification-layers-and-kinds)
- [Sizing: byte budgets](#sizing-byte-budgets)
- [Artifacts: what gets written](#artifacts-what-gets-written)
- [Secrets: redaction](#secrets-redaction)
- [Output directories](#output-directories)
- [Hooks](#hooks)
- [Complete example](#complete-example)
- [Interactive setup](#interactive-setup)

## Config files

The first match wins:

```
snapshot.config.ts
snapshot.config.js
snapshot.config.mjs
snapshot.config.cjs
snapshot.config.json
.snapshotrc / .snapshotrc.json
package.json → "snapshot"
```

`snapshot.config.json` accepts comments. `package.json`'s `"snapshot"` key is read only when
no dedicated config file exists, and it must be an object (a wrong type is a warning, since
other tools use that key too).

The extension decides how the file is loaded. `.cjs` is always CommonJS and must use
`module.exports`; `.mjs` and `.ts` are always ESM and must use `export default`. `.js` follows
the package's `type` field — `module` means ESM, `commonjs` means CommonJS; with no `type`
Node decides by detecting the syntax, so `export default` works on current Node but is not
portable to versions without detection. Getting it wrong is the `Could not load …` error
above, not a silent fallback.

A config file that exists but cannot be parsed is a **hard error** (exit `1`, nothing
written). Silently ignoring it would produce a snapshot with none of your settings.

A `.ts` config needs Node ≥ 22.6 **and** an ES-module project, because Node applies the
package's `type` field to a `.ts` file. `codebase-snapshot init` picks the right filename for
the project — `.ts` when both conditions hold, `.mjs` otherwise — and writes a plain object
with no imports so it loads even when the package is only used through `npx`.

The snippets below show an `export default { … }` object for brevity. `init` writes it as
`const config = { … }; export default config;` because the anonymous form is reported by
`import/no-anonymous-default-export`, which `eslint-config-next` enables by default — both load
identically, so use whichever keeps your linter quiet.

CLI flags override the config for a single run (`--max-part-bytes`, `--out-dir`, …).
`--no-config` goes further and ignores every config file, so the run uses the built-in
defaults and the project's detected preset. A code config is executed when it is loaded, so
`--no-config` is also the way to snapshot a repository whose config you do not want to run —
see [the trust boundary](../SECURITY.md#trust-boundary).

## Scoping: which files get in

```js
export default {
  include: [],                    // allow-list; empty means "everything"
  excludePatterns: [],            // extra excludes, applied on top of the preset's
  discovery: "auto",              // "auto" | "git" | "walk" — how files are found
};
```

- `include` is an allow-list of globs. When non-empty, only matching files are considered.
- `exclude` is accepted as an alias of `excludePatterns` and means the same thing; the resolved
  config always records it under `excludePatterns`.
- `excludePatterns` removes files everywhere. Presets contribute their own ecosystem ignores
  on top; those are not removable, only addable to.
- `discovery` chooses how the file list is produced, and is recorded in `manifest.config`, so
  changing it shows up as config drift:
  - `"auto"` (default) — list through `git ls-files` when git can do it; if a `.git` directory
    exists but git cannot list the files, refuse rather than silently switch methods.
  - `"git"` — require git; a directory without a usable repository is an error.
  - `"walk"` — scan the tree and read `.gitignore` files directly (nested ones included), still
    skipping credential files. Use it when a `.git` directory cannot be listed. It cannot
    reproduce git's own ignore sources (`.git/info/exclude`, your global `core.excludesFile`),
    git's inclusion of files that are tracked although a `.gitignore` now matches them, or its
    treatment of a submodule as a single entry. The same setting is available per run as
    [`--discovery`](cli.md#options).

Built-in ignores always apply: `.git`, `node_modules`, `.next`, `dist`, `build`, lockfiles,
minified assets and binaries. Sensitive files are handled separately (see
[Secrets](#secrets-redaction)).

Every path the scanner discovers is recorded in `manifest.skipped[]` with the reason and the
rule that matched — nothing it sees is dropped silently, including a `.gitignore`d path in a
repository where git does the filtering. See the [`skipped[]`
reasons](output.md#skipped). To debug one path:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot explain src/some/file.ts
```

## Project presets

Detection reads the repository **once** and picks the highest-priority match, so a Next.js
app inside a pnpm workspace is still `nextjs`, not `monorepo`. `peerDependencies` and
`optionalDependencies` count as dependencies. A bare `package.json` ranks below any
definitive language manifest, so a Go or Rust repository with a `package.json` for tooling is
not mistaken for a Node library.

| Preset | Priority | Detected from | Budget | Ecosystem ignores |
| :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `nextjs` | 100 | `next.config.*`, or `next` | 300k | `.svelte-kit`, `.astro`, `.vite`, `.output` |
| `nuxt` | 98 | `nuxt.config.*`, or `nuxt` | 300k | `.nuxt`, `.output` |
| `sveltekit` | 96 | `svelte.config.*`, `@sveltejs/kit` | 300k | `.svelte-kit`, `.vercel` |
| `remix` | 94 | `@remix-run/*` | 300k | `.cache`, `public/build` |
| `astro` | 92 | `astro.config.*`, `astro` | 300k | `.astro`, `dist` |
| `angular` | 90 | `angular.json`, `@angular/core` | 300k | `.angular`, `dist` |
| `solid` | 88 | `solid-js`, `solid-start` | 300k | `.solid`, `dist` |
| `vue-vite` | 86 | `vue` + `vite`/`vite.config.*` | 300k | `.vite`, `dist` |
| `react-vite` | 84 | `react` + `vite`/`vite.config.*` | 300k | `.vite`, `dist` |
| `nest` | 80 | `@nestjs/core` | 300k | `dist`, `node_modules` |
| `node-express` | 78 | `express`, `fastify`, `koa`, `@hapi/hapi` | 300k | `dist` |
| `monorepo` | 70 | `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `nx.json`, `turbo.json`, `lerna.json`, `"workspaces"` | 300k | `.turbo`, `.nx` |
| `python-django` | 56 | `manage.py`, django in `pyproject.toml`/`requirements.txt` | 300k | `__pycache__`, `.venv`, `venv`, `.mypy_cache`, `.pytest_cache`, `.tox`, `.eggs`, `staticfiles`, `media` |
| `python-fastapi` | 54 | fastapi or flask in `pyproject.toml`/`requirements.txt` | 300k | `__pycache__`, `.venv`, `venv`, `.mypy_cache`, `.pytest_cache`, `.tox`, `.eggs` |
| `go` | 50 | `go.mod` | 300k | `vendor`, `testdata` |
| `rust` | 48 | `Cargo.toml` | 300k | `target` |
| `java-spring` | 46 | `pom.xml`, `build.gradle`, `build.gradle.kts` | 300k | `.gradle`, `out`, `target` |
| `node-lib` | 45 | any `package.json` | 300k | `dist` |
| `dotnet` | 44 | `*.csproj`, `*.sln`, `global.json` | 300k | `bin`, `obj`, `.vs` |
| `php-laravel` | 42 | `artisan`, laravel in `composer.json`, `composer.json` | 300k | `vendor`, `storage`, `bootstrap/cache`, `public/build` |
| `ruby-rails` | 40 | `Gemfile`, `config.ru`, `Rakefile` | 300k | `.bundle`, `log`, `tmp`, `vendor/bundle`, `public/assets` |
| `python-lib` | 30 | `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py`, `requirements.txt`, `.py` files | 300k | `__pycache__`, `.venv`, `venv`, `.mypy_cache`, `.pytest_cache`, `.tox`, `.eggs` |
| `infra` | 24 | `*.tf`, `Chart.yaml`, `kustomization.yaml`, `docker-compose.yml` | 300k | `.terraform`, `.terragrunt-cache` |
| `docs` | 22 | ≥ 3 markdown files and no `package.json` | 300k | `.docusaurus`, `.vitepress/dist`, `site` |
| `generic` | 0 | anything else | 300k | — |

Each detector reports **the signal that fired**, not just a verdict: `next.config.ts`, `depends on
express`, `django in pyproject.toml`. The first run prints it — `Detected nextjs` / `Because
next.config.ts` — so a detection can be checked rather than taken on faith. `generic` is the
fallback, reached only when no detector matched, and the first run says so instead of pretending it
recognised the project.

A preset contributes layers, kinds, ignores and a recommended budget — what `init` writes as
`maxPartBytes`. Force one when detection is wrong:

```js
export default { preset: "react-vite" };
```

An unknown name is an error listing every valid preset, never a silent fallback. The resolved
preset is recorded in `manifest.config.preset`, so changing it shows up as config drift.

## Classification: layers and kinds

Layers group files (roughly directories), kinds label their role. Both are first-match-wins
with `other` as the fallback, and both accept a glob string, a `RegExp`, or a function.

```js
export default {
  layersMode: "merge",         // "merge" (default) or "replace"
  layers: [
    { name: "api", match: "src/api/**" },
    { name: "ui", match: /\.(tsx|vue|svelte)$/ },
  ],
  kinds: [
    { kind: "route", match: /(^|\/)route\.tsx?$/ },
    { kind: "hook", match: "src/hooks/**" },
  ],
};
```

A layer rule is `{ name, match }` and a kind rule is `{ kind, match }`. The label key is
different in each, and a row that gets it wrong is rejected rather than silently ignored, so
copy the shapes above.

- `layersMode: "merge"` keeps the preset's rules and lets yours win by being evaluated first.
- `layersMode: "replace"` discards the preset's rules entirely.

Classification drives per-layer budgets and the `Layers:` breakdown in `report`.

## Sizing: byte budgets

```js
export default {
  maxPartBytes: "300k",        // hard ceiling per part; default 307200, minimum 4096
  // maxPartTokens: 32000,     // a per-part ceiling in tokens; narrows maxPartBytes (tokens x 4,
  //                           // and the byte ceiling is what is enforced — see docs/cli.md)
  // changedSince: "main",      // only files changed since this git ref
  // staged: true,             // only files staged for the next commit
  // withDependents: true,     // ...and the files that import them
  // skeleton: true,           // signatures instead of bodies (--skeleton); TS, JS, TSX and JSX
  largeFileBytes: "300k",      // above this a file becomes a head/tail preview; defaults to
                               // maxPartBytes, and may not exceed it
  previewHeadLines: 80,
  previewTailLines: 40,
  budgets: {                   // optional per-layer overrides
    default: "120k",
    byLayer: { docs: 40_000, core: 300_000 },
  },
};
```

Sizes accept bytes (`307200`), `"120k"`, `"1.5mb"`, and spaces (`"120 k"`). Zero and negative
values are errors; so is anything below 4096 — a part budget that small cannot hold a header,
let alone code.

The budget is a hard ceiling: a part never exceeds it. A single file larger than the budget is
not split mid-file — it becomes a head/tail preview, marked `isPreviewTruncated`, and listed in
`manifest.incomplete[]` with the line ranges it lost.

`largeFileBytes` is the size at which that happens. It defaults to the part budget and may not
be set above it: a file between the two could not fit a part, so it would be cut to fit while
the manifest called it a preview by policy. That combination is a config error.

`budgets.byLayer` lets expensive layers get more room than generated docs, for example.

## Artifacts: what gets written

```js
export default {
  artifacts: {
    readme: true,          // snapshot/README.md
    prompt: true,          // snapshot/PROMPT.md — the instruction sheet a model is given
    manifest: true,        // snapshot/manifest.json
    parts: true,           // snapshot/parts/*.md
    navigation: true,      // snapshot/reports/navigation.md
    projectTree: true,     // snapshot/reports/project-tree.md
    audit: true,           // snapshot/reports/audit.md
    monolith: false,       // full-codebase.json + full-codebase.md
  },
};
```

Everything except `monolith` is on by default; `monolith` follows `includeMonolith`. Turn
artifacts off with the config or `--skip-artifacts a,b`. `--only-artifacts a,b` is different: it
*writes only* those and turns each named artifact **on**, so `--only-artifacts audit` still produces
`reports/audit.md` in a project whose config says `audit: false`. The two flags cannot be combined.

Two combinations warn because they produce a snapshot that contradicts itself: `manifest` off
means `validate` and `report` cannot inspect the snapshot, and `parts` off with `navigation`
on leaves links pointing at files that were never written. Both are allowed.

The `.codebase-snapshot` ownership marker is always written, whatever the selection.

## Delivering it: how many files the destination takes

```js
export default {
  attachmentSlots: 10,   // files your chat accepts per message; 0 = no limit
};
```

Hosted assistants take a fixed number of files per message — 10 is the common cap — and every part
is one of them. `PROMPT.md` plans the delivery against this number: which parts to attach, what to
leave behind when they do not all fit, and the `--max-part-bytes` value that would merge them.

- Default `10`; `0` means the destination imposes no limit and the plan says to attach everything.
- It changes **no part and no file**: the payload is byte-identical either way. Only the delivery
  section of `PROMPT.md` moves, which is why it is recorded in `manifest.config` but is not part of
  the config digest — a new delivery preference must not make an existing snapshot fail `validate`.
- Instructions are pasted as text and cost no slot, so the budget is always spent on files.

The one thing it cannot do is make a snapshot fit: a 12-part snapshot does not go into a 10-file
message at any setting. Raise `maxPartBytes` to merge the parts, or narrow the scope — the plan in
`PROMPT.md` names the number.

### Experimental: a fixed number of parts

```js
export default {
  maxParts: 10,   // grow the parts until the snapshot has at most ten; off by default
};
```

The opposite trade to everything above: the **count** is fixed and the **size** gives way. As the
repository grows, `maxParts` grows the per-part budget instead of adding a part, which is what a
destination with a hard file limit wants — the snapshot keeps its shape instead of turning into
eleven parts next month.

- Off by default (`null`). Opt in per run with `--max-parts <n>`, or in the config.
- The budget it settles on is recorded in `manifest.budgets.maxPartBytes` — the number the parts
  actually honour — while `manifest.config.maxPartBytes` keeps the configured value, so
  `validate` still compares like with like.
- It may exceed `maxPartBytes`, and that is the point: a part bigger than the configured ceiling is
  more tokens at once, and some destinations cap a single file. Both facts land in
  `manifest.warnings`, so the run says what it did rather than quietly changing the shape.
- If it cannot converge, generation fails with the counts it tried rather than writing something
  the setting did not ask for.

Treat it as experimental: `maxPartBytes` stays the honest control, and this is the knob for the
case where the file count, not the byte count, is the binding constraint.

## The brief: what only you can write

[The brief](brief.md) documents the format in full: the merge, the refusals, and how the file is
parsed.

`PROMPT.md` is generated, and half of it cannot be: what the software is *for*, what has already
gone wrong, and which failures you would recognise. That is judgement, so it is supplied as a
file and merged at fixed slots.

```js
export default {
  brief: "docs/brief.md",   // relative to cwd; a missing file is an error
  promptTask: "audit",      // audit | review | explain
};
```

The file is plain markdown with five recognised headings:

| Heading | What happens |
| :--- | :--- |
| `## Stakes` | rendered as its own section, right after the job |
| `## The system` | replaces the generated narrative, and the index facts follow under "What the index records" |
| `## What came before` | rendered above the generated commit ledger |
| `## The bar` | replaces the standard |
| `## Calibration` | replaces the example failures |

```markdown
<!-- docs/brief.md -->
## Stakes
Runs live production for one owner. A wrong cleanup deletes their data.

## What came before
Four rounds, 23 defects. The recurring shapes are a guard at the route but not the timer, and
an ok flag that means the call returned.

## Calibration
- a grant signed for one recipe executing another recipe's deletions
- a recipe that never worked, because its test supplied a number where the daemon sends a string
```

Any other `##` heading is kept, verbatim, under **Owner notes** — nothing is dropped because the
merge did not recognise it. Every owner section is marked in the output as written by the project
owner, so a reader can tell an assertion from a measurement.

A brief is capped at 64 KiB: past that it is a second codebase rather than instruction, and
the error names the size. Text inside a fenced code block is content — a sample `## Coverage` in a
documentation example is not a section — and `## Stakes:` is read as `## Stakes`, because the
colon is a typo rather than a different heading.

Three things are refused because they would make the document lie:

- a heading that redefines a **measured** section (`Coverage`, `This snapshot`, `Attaching this`,
  `The job`, `What you have`, `What a useful answer contains`, `Your task`) is a configuration
  error, not an override;
- a `brief` path that cannot be read is an error — a snapshot must not silently ship without the
  half you wrote;
- and the file is digested into the config digest, so editing the brief after generating makes
  `validate` report drift. A stale brief is the same failure as a stale snapshot.

`promptTask` changes only the opening section: `audit` (the default) asks for what is wrong or
will break, `review` for what a change breaks, `explain` for how a subsystem works. The standard
and the coverage duty are the same in all three.

See [Snapshot output](output.md) for the layout and the manifest schema.

## Secrets: redaction

```js
export default {
  secrets: {
    customPatterns: [
      { rule: "github-pat", pattern: /ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}/ },
      { rule: "internal-token", pattern: /CORP-[A-Z0-9]{32}/ },
    ],
    disableDefaultPatterns: false,
  },
  includeSensitiveFiles: false,   // .env, *.pem, .npmrc, .ssh/, .tfstate …
};
```

Each entry is `{ rule, pattern }`, where `rule` is the name that appears in the audit and
`pattern` is a `RegExp`, or a string in a `.json` config. A bare `RegExp` in the array works
too — it comes out as `custom-1`, `custom-2`, … in the findings — but naming the rule is what
makes an audit entry mean something.

- Credential-bearing **files** are skipped by default and listed in `skipped[]` with reason
  `sensitive-file`. `includeSensitiveFiles: true` emits them.
- Secret-shaped **strings** inside ordinary files are redacted in the emitted parts. Only the
  matched span is masked, never the whole line, and no excerpt of a matched line reaches any
  artifact. Multi-line PEM keys are masked as a block.
- `customPatterns` add rules; `disableDefaultPatterns` removes the built-in set — it does not
  disable redaction itself, and it applies to **paths** as well as to content, so a
  credential-shaped file name is no longer skipped by a rule you switched off.

Redaction is **fail-closed**, so most placeholders are indistinguishable from secrets: a
`.env.example` whose line reads `JWT_SECRET=replace-with-base64url-48-bytes` is masked like a
live key would be, and a test fixture literal such as `process.env.X_SECRET = "test-value"` is
masked too. That is the intended trade-off — the alternative is guessing which values are
throwaway — and it is worth knowing before you hand the snapshot to a model and find the template
less readable than the original. To narrow it, remove a rule by name with
`disableDefaultPatterns: true` plus your own `customPatterns`.

The exception is a **whole value** that is one of the conventional placeholder shapes. The list
is exhaustive and lives in `PLACEHOLDER_PATTERNS` in `src/security/redact.ts`:

| Value is exactly… | Example that survives |
| :--- | :--- |
| `replace-me` / `change-me`, optionally followed by more words | `replace-me-with-the-read-tier-key` |
| `your-…` / `your_…` | `your-api-key-here`, `your_api_key` |
| `example` / `sample`, optionally followed by more words | `example-token`, `sample-value` |
| a single `<…>` (the closing bracket may be missing) | `<fine-grained PAT>`, `<missing-bracket` |
| three or more dots | `...`, `sk-...` |
| four or more `x`, `X` or `*` | `xxxx`, `****` |

Everything else is masked, including `replace-with-…` — the rule keys on the `-me` — and any real
credential that merely contains the word "example". This list is the only exemption; there is no
entropy heuristic behind it.

A second shape that is *not* masked is a value that is a single `${…}` reference and nothing else —
`password: "${DB_PASSWORD}"`, `` `${process.env.TOKEN}` `` — because that is an expression and
masking it rewrites a program (or an assertion message) rather than hiding a credential. Anything
inside the braces that is not a plain reference settles it the other way:
`password: "${DB_PASSWORD:-hunter2}"` is masked, because the shell and Compose idiom for a default
puts a **hardcoded credential** in the fallback position. So does a second interpolation, or any
quote inside the braces.

The same default is masked when it is not quoted, which is how it is usually written:

```bash
# .env, docker-compose.yml, a workflow, a shell script — all of these are masked
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-MyProdPassword123!}
POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-MyProdPassword123!}
```

The fallback is what gets replaced; the variable name stays, exactly as `POSTGRES_PASSWORD:` does.
`${PORT:-3000}` is untouched: the name is not a credential name, and a port number is not one
either.

A credential assigned to a name inside a string is also masked — a connection string is a string:

```js
const dsn = "password=MySuperSecret123;host=db.internal";
```

What is left alone is a credential *name* in a literal, with nothing assigned to it:
`["awsSecret:", "apiKey:"]` is a list of keys, not two credentials, and a documentation line showing
`password: "…"` inside a code span is showing an example. The difference is whether the literal
closes between the name and the value.

`readonly` and `declare` are shell assignments in a shell file and declarations in a source file, and
the file decides: `readonly api_key=hunter2` in `.envrc`, `.sh`, `.zsh`, a YAML or an INI file is
masked, while `private readonly apiKey = someValue` in TypeScript is left alone.

The file type decides how an **unquoted** value is read too. In a source file (`.ts`, `.js`, `.tsx`,
…) a bare value after a credential name is an expression, and expressions are left as written:
`opts.token: (value)` and `password: getPassword() & 0xff` are untouched. In a data or shell file
(`.yaml`, `.yml`, `.toml`, `.ini`, `.conf`, `.cfg`, `.properties`, `.tf`, `.tfvars`, `.sh`,
`.env` and the `.envrc` family) the same line is an assignment: a **strong** credential name in
colon form (`password:`, `token:`, `api_key:`) masks the value whatever symbols it holds — a
`password` value such as `pa&ss`, or an expression like `getPassword() & 0xff`, in a `.yaml` is
masked whole, running to the end of the line (a trailing `#` comment is kept, and the match is
capped at 300 characters).
A value that cannot be bounded is handled by file type: in a data or shell file a colon-form
assignment still masks the value whole, while in source code the unbounded match is dropped rather
than emitted as a marker in front of an expression. The `=` form follows the same file-type rule:
in a data or shell file a key ending in a credential word (`password`, `client_secret`, `access_key`,
`apiKey`, upper or lower case) masks the whole value whatever symbols it contains, while the same
line in source code is left alone unless it matches an existing source rule. A key that merely
contains a credential word (`tokenCount`, `secretary`) is not a credential and is untouched.

Findings are summarised in `reports/audit.md` (one row per line, naming every rule that matched
it), counted in `manifest.totals.redactedLines`, and shown in the `generate` summary as a
`Redacted:` line separate from `Warnings:`.

## Output directories

```js
export default {
  outDir: "snapshot",
  tmpDir: "snapshot.tmp",
};
```

`outDir` may not be the working directory, an ancestor, or the filesystem root, and
`outDir`/`tmpDir` may not contain one another — the message prints the resolved paths. Writes
are atomic: the previous snapshot survives until the new one is complete.

Both directories are also **refused if they already exist and this generator did not write
them**, so `outDir: "src"` cannot replace your source tree. Ownership is proved by the
`.codebase-snapshot` marker and nothing else: a JSON object naming this generator, with a
schema version this build understands, a per-snapshot `id`, and the list of entries it wrote.
A file with that name but no valid marker, or a directory holding a manifest from another
tool, is refused. The proof is never a filename, so an existing `docs/README.md` is safe.

Files your hook writes into the output directory are recorded in the marker's entry list, so
they do not block later runs; a file you add by hand afterwards does, because it is not in the
snapshot the marker describes.

## Hooks

```js
export default {
  hooks: {
    afterGenerate: (manifest) => {
      // runs after a real generate; a dry run does not call it
    },
  },
};
```

A throwing hook is downgraded to a warning on the manifest — it never loses a snapshot that
was already written. A hook may return a promise: it is not awaited (this API is synchronous),
and a rejection is recorded as a warning on the re-shipped manifest and marker. Anything the
hook writes to stdout is redirected to stderr for the duration of the call, so `--format json`
stays parseable.

"Not awaited" does not mean "free": Node keeps the process alive while the hook's own timers,
sockets or pending I/O are outstanding, so a hook that schedules a long job delays the command by
that long even though the snapshot is already written. Keep `afterGenerate` short — hand long work
to a background process — or use the programmatic `generateSnapshot`, whose promise you can `await`
alongside your own work.

A hook may write its own files into the output directory; that is safe across reruns because
ownership is established by the marker rather than by the filenames present.

A hook runs in the **process** working directory, so with `--cwd elsewhere` a relative path
resolves against your shell, not the target repository. Use the absolute paths on `manifest`
(or resolve against `outDir` yourself) when passing `--cwd`.

While an **async** hook is still running, the run keeps both locks (the staging root and the
output directory) until it settles. A second `generate` for the same output is refused with
"Another generate run is already using…" instead of publishing over the hook's writes; the lock's
heartbeat means a hook that never settles cannot wedge the directory forever.

A module config is re-evaluated only when its own bytes change. A helper module it imports stays
cached under its own stable URL, so editing only the helper is picked up on the next process start
— edit the config file itself if a running `--watch` must see the change.

A `match` **function** in a layer or kind rule is executable code, and `manifest.config.digest`
can only cover its source text, not the values its closure captures. `validate` says so in a
warning rather than claiming that the classification is unchanged; use a string or `RegExp`
matcher if the digest's answer matters to you.

## Complete example

Every setting at its built-in default. Nothing here has to be written — the tool runs on these
values with no config at all.

```js
const config = {
  // ── Project ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  preset: "detected",            // "detected" auto-detects; or name one of the 25
  branch: "main",                // recorded when git cannot report one

  // ── Output ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  outDir: "snapshot",
  tmpDir: "snapshot.tmp",

  // ── Size ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  maxPartBytes: "300k",
  maxPartTokens: null,           // the same ceiling in tokens; narrows maxPartBytes
  largeFileBytes: "300k",
  // ^ defaults to maxPartBytes. A threshold above the part budget is a config error: a file
  //   between the two cannot fit a part, so it would be cut while the manifest called it a
  //   preview by policy. Previewing exists for files that cannot be emitted whole.
  previewHeadLines: 80,
  previewTailLines: 40,
  budgets: { default: "300k", byLayer: {} },

  // ── Scope and compression ────────────────────────────────
  skeleton: false,               // replace function bodies with { /* ... */ }
  sarif: false,                  // also write reports/audit.sarif (SARIF 2.1.0)
  changedSince: null,            // only files changed since this git ref
  staged: false,                 // only what is staged in the index
  withDependents: false,         // with a scope, also include one-hop importers

  // ── What goes in ─────────────────────────────────────────
  include: [],
  excludePatterns: [],
  discovery: "auto",                 // "auto" | "git" | "walk"

  // ── Delivery ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  attachmentSlots: 10,               // files your destination takes per message; 0 = no limit

  // ── Classification ───────────────────────────────────────
  layersMode: "merge",
  layers: [],
  kinds: [],

  // ── Safety ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  includeSensitiveFiles: false,
  secrets: { customPatterns: [], disableDefaultPatterns: false },

  // ── Artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────
  artifacts: {
    readme: true,
    prompt: true,
    manifest: true,
    parts: true,
    navigation: true,
    projectTree: true,
    audit: true,
    monolith: false,
  },
  maxMonolithBytes: null,        // skip the monolith above this size
  includeMonolith: false,        // top-level switch; the artifacts entry wins

  // ── Advanced ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  hooks: {},
};

export default config;
```

Deleting any line is safe — the defaults are real, and each one is what the comment says.

The object is exported through a name because `export default { … }` is reported by
`import/no-anonymous-default-export`, which `eslint-config-next` enables by default: the inline
form made the generated file a lint error on first save in a Next.js project. A named export is
also what lets you reference the object (from a test, or from `--dry-run`) without a wrapper.

This is **not** byte-identical to a generated file. `codebase-snapshot init` starts from these
values and substitutes two things: `preset` becomes the detected name (`node-lib` for a plain
npm package), and `maxPartBytes` with `budgets.default` become that preset's
`recommendedBudget` (`"300k"` for every preset). `generate` on its own applies no
recommended budget — without a config it uses `--max-part-bytes`, default 307200 — so the
preset's figure only takes effect once `init` has written it into a config.

## Interactive setup

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot init
```

Walks every tunable parameter, showing the detected value as the default — press `enter` through
the lot and you get the recommended configuration.

The bare `snapshot` command starts the same wizard when the project has no config, but asks one
question first: take the recommended setup in a single keystroke, walk through each setting, or pick
a different preset. Every prompt has a flag, so the same setup works non-interactively:

```bash
npx codebase-snapshot init --yes
npx codebase-snapshot init --preset go --max-part-bytes 200k \
    --include "cmd/**,internal/**" --exclude "**/*_test.go" --yes
```

`--dry-run` prints the config and writes nothing. `--force` replaces an existing ES-module
config (`.ts`, `.js`, `.mjs`) in place; without it, an existing config is left alone and the
command exits `1`.

It will not replace a JSON-family config — `snapshot.config.json`, `.snapshotrc`,
`.snapshotrc.json`, or the `"snapshot"` key in `package.json` — because this wizard writes an
ES-module file and a new file beside the old one would silently shadow it. Those cases exit `1`
with a message naming the file to delete or rename. Note that this means `--force` writing
`snapshot.config.ts` does shadow a `"snapshot"` key in `package.json`, since a dedicated config
file always wins.
